DECLARE course jsonb := $course$[{"key":"a1-identity","level":"A1","title":"People and introductions","objective":"Introduce a person and understand basic personal information.","project_prompt":"Write 40–80 words about yourself or a familiar person, using the unit situation as a model. Include clear basic facts and simple linked sentences.\nTopic: People and introductions\nFocus: Introduce a person and understand basic personal information.\nSource context: Hello! My name is Eva. I am nineteen. I am from Mexico, but my friend Leo is from Chile. We are students. Our English teacher is Anna. Her classroom is small. We study on Monday and Wednesday. Leo and I are in the same class, but we are not from the same country.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use I am, you are, and he or she is. My, your and her show who something belongs to. Ask What is your name? and Where are you from? A country is not a nationality: Mexico is a country; Mexican describes a person from Mexico.","description":"Introduce a person and understand basic personal information. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Where is Eva from?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Chile"},{"id":"o1","label":"Mexico"},{"id":"o2","label":"England"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Eva says she is from Mexico; Leo is from Chile.","passage":"Hello! My name is Eva. I am nineteen. I am from Mexico, but my friend Leo is from Chile. We are students. Our English teacher is Anna. Her classroom is small. We study on Monday and Wednesday. Leo and I are in the same class, but we are not from the same country.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What do Eva and Leo share?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Their country"},{"id":"o1","label":"Their age"},{"id":"o2","label":"Their English class"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"They are in the same class; Leo's age is not stated.","passage":"Hello! My name is Eva. I am nineteen. I am from Mexico, but my friend Leo is from Chile. We are students. Our English teacher is Anna. Her classroom is small. We study on Monday and Wednesday. Leo and I are in the same class, but we are not from the same country.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use I am, you are, and he or she is. My, your and her show who something belongs to. Ask What is your name? and Where are you from? A country is not a nationality: Mexico is a country; Mexican describes a person from Mexico.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with am, is or are: My name ___ Eva.","solution":"is","explanation":"Name is singular, so use is.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Rewrite with she: I am a student.","solution":"She is a student.","explanation":"Change I to she and am to is.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use I am, you are, and he or she is. My, your and her show who something belongs to. Ask What is your name? and Where are you from? A country is not a nationality: Mexico is a country; Mexican describes a person from Mexico.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which response introduces you?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Nice to meet you. I am Rosa."},{"id":"o1","label":"You are from Monday."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, the room is small."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Give your name and acknowledge the introduction.","passage":"A new student joins your class. Student: Hello, I am Ben. What is your name? You want to introduce yourself politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use I am, you are, and he or she is. My, your and her show who something belongs to. Ask What is your name? and Where are you from? A country is not a nationality: Mexico is a country; Mexican describes a person from Mexico.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"How old is Sam?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Twelve"},{"id":"o1","label":"Twenty"},{"id":"o2","label":"Thirty"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Sam says: I am twenty.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-identity"},{"prompt":"When is Sam's class?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Thursday"},{"id":"o1","label":"Monday"},{"id":"o2","label":"Tuesday"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"He says Tuesday and explicitly rules out Thursday.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-identity"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Where is Eva from?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Chile"},{"id":"o1","label":"Mexico"},{"id":"o2","label":"England"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Eva says she is from Mexico; Leo is from Chile.","passage":"Hello! My name is Eva. I am nineteen. I am from Mexico, but my friend Leo is from Chile. We are students. Our English teacher is Anna. Her classroom is small. We study on Monday and Wednesday. Leo and I are in the same class, but we are not from the same country.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What do Eva and Leo share?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Their country"},{"id":"o1","label":"Their age"},{"id":"o2","label":"Their English class"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"They are in the same class; Leo's age is not stated.","passage":"Hello! My name is Eva. I am nineteen. I am from Mexico, but my friend Leo is from Chile. We are students. Our English teacher is Anna. Her classroom is small. We study on Monday and Wednesday. Leo and I are in the same class, but we are not from the same country.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which response introduces you?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Nice to meet you. I am Rosa."},{"id":"o1","label":"You are from Monday."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, the room is small."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Give your name and acknowledge the introduction.","passage":"A new student joins your class. Student: Hello, I am Ben. What is your name? You want to introduce yourself politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use I am, you are, and he or she is. My, your and her show who something belongs to. Ask What is your name? and Where are you from? A country is not a nationality: Mexico is a country; Mexican describes a person from Mexico.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a1-routines","level":"A1","title":"Days and daily routines","objective":"Understand simple routines, clock times and present-simple forms.","project_prompt":"Write 40–80 words about yourself or a familiar person, using the unit situation as a model. Include clear basic facts and simple linked sentences.\nTopic: Days and daily routines\nFocus: Understand simple routines, clock times and present-simple forms.\nSource context: Nora works in a cafe. She starts at eight in the morning. She has lunch at one and finishes at four. On Saturday, she does not work. She visits her brother after breakfast. They walk to the park. On Sunday, Nora stays at home and reads a book.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use the present simple for routines. With I, say I work; with she, say she works. Use at with clock times and on with days. Do not confuse a routine with an action happening now. Before means earlier; after means later.","description":"Understand simple routines, clock times and present-simple forms. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"When does Nora finish work?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At eight"},{"id":"o1","label":"At four"},{"id":"o2","label":"At one"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She finishes at four; eight is her start time.","passage":"Nora works in a cafe. She starts at eight in the morning. She has lunch at one and finishes at four. On Saturday, she does not work. She visits her brother after breakfast. They walk to the park. On Sunday, Nora stays at home and reads a book.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What does she do on Saturday?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"She visits her brother."},{"id":"o1","label":"She works in the cafe."},{"id":"o2","label":"She reads at home all day."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The Saturday routine includes visiting her brother after breakfast.","passage":"Nora works in a cafe. She starts at eight in the morning. She has lunch at one and finishes at four. On Saturday, she does not work. She visits her brother after breakfast. They walk to the park. On Sunday, Nora stays at home and reads a book.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the present simple for routines. With I, say I work; with she, say she works. Use at with clock times and on with days. Do not confuse a routine with an action happening now. Before means earlier; after means later.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: She ___ (work) in a cafe.","solution":"works","explanation":"Add -s with she in an affirmative present-simple sentence.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete with at or on: The class starts ___ nine.","solution":"at","explanation":"Use at with a clock time.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the present simple for routines. With I, say I work; with she, say she works. Use at with clock times and on with days. Do not confuse a routine with an action happening now. Before means earlier; after means later.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the useful reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Yes, I work every Friday."},{"id":"o1","label":"I cannot on Friday. Is Saturday OK?"},{"id":"o2","label":"Friday is a number."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Decline the unavailable day and offer a specific alternative.","passage":"You arrange a weekly study session. Friend: Are you free on Friday? You work on Friday, but you are free on Saturday.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the present simple for routines. With I, say I work; with she, say she works. Use at with clock times and on with days. Do not confuse a routine with an action happening now. Before means earlier; after means later.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Where does the speaker eat on Friday?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At home"},{"id":"o1","label":"On the train"},{"id":"o2","label":"At the station"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"Friday is an exception to the usual breakfast at home.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-routines"},{"prompt":"When does the train leave?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At seven"},{"id":"o1","label":"At eight thirty"},{"id":"o2","label":"At eight"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The train leaves at eight thirty.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-routines"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"When does Nora finish work?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At eight"},{"id":"o1","label":"At four"},{"id":"o2","label":"At one"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She finishes at four; eight is her start time.","passage":"Nora works in a cafe. She starts at eight in the morning. She has lunch at one and finishes at four. On Saturday, she does not work. She visits her brother after breakfast. They walk to the park. On Sunday, Nora stays at home and reads a book.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What does she do on Saturday?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"She visits her brother."},{"id":"o1","label":"She works in the cafe."},{"id":"o2","label":"She reads at home all day."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The Saturday routine includes visiting her brother after breakfast.","passage":"Nora works in a cafe. She starts at eight in the morning. She has lunch at one and finishes at four. On Saturday, she does not work. She visits her brother after breakfast. They walk to the park. On Sunday, Nora stays at home and reads a book.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the useful reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Yes, I work every Friday."},{"id":"o1","label":"I cannot on Friday. Is Saturday OK?"},{"id":"o2","label":"Friday is a number."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Decline the unavailable day and offer a specific alternative.","passage":"You arrange a weekly study session. Friend: Are you free on Friday? You work on Friday, but you are free on Saturday.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use the present simple for routines. With I, say I work; with she, say she works. Use at with clock times and on with days. Do not confuse a routine with an action happening now. Before means earlier; after means later.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a1-places","level":"A1","title":"Homes and neighbourhoods","objective":"Locate everyday places and understand there is and there are.","project_prompt":"Write 40–80 words about yourself or a familiar person, using the unit situation as a model. Include clear basic facts and simple linked sentences.\nTopic: Homes and neighbourhoods\nFocus: Locate everyday places and understand there is and there are.\nSource context: Our street has a library, a small shop and a park. The library is opposite the shop. The park is behind the library. There are two benches in the park, but there is no cafe. My flat is next to the shop. I walk to the library because it is near my home.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use there is for one thing and there are for more than one. Next to means beside. Opposite means across from. Between needs two reference points. A location tells us where something is; a direction tells us how to get there.","description":"Locate everyday places and understand there is and there are. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What is opposite the library?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The shop"},{"id":"o1","label":"The park"},{"id":"o2","label":"A cafe"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The library is opposite the shop.","passage":"Our street has a library, a small shop and a park. The library is opposite the shop. The park is behind the library. There are two benches in the park, but there is no cafe. My flat is next to the shop. I walk to the library because it is near my home.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is not in the park?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Two benches"},{"id":"o1","label":"A cafe"},{"id":"o2","label":"A path to the library"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The text explicitly says there is no cafe.","passage":"Our street has a library, a small shop and a park. The library is opposite the shop. The park is behind the library. There are two benches in the park, but there is no cafe. My flat is next to the shop. I walk to the library because it is near my home.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use there is for one thing and there are for more than one. Next to means beside. Opposite means across from. Between needs two reference points. A location tells us where something is; a direction tells us how to get there.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with is or are: There ___ two benches.","solution":"are","explanation":"Two benches is plural.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete with is or are: There ___ a shop near my home.","solution":"is","explanation":"A shop is singular.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use there is for one thing and there are for more than one. Next to means beside. Opposite means across from. Between needs two reference points. A location tells us where something is; a direction tells us how to get there.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose a helpful direction.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It opens on Tuesday."},{"id":"o1","label":"It is opposite the shop."},{"id":"o2","label":"I like reading books."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Opposite the shop gives the requested location.","passage":"Visitor: Excuse me, where is the library? You know it is across the street from the shop.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use there is for one thing and there are for more than one. Next to means beside. Opposite means across from. Between needs two reference points. A location tells us where something is; a direction tells us how to get there.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Where is the pharmacy?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Behind the supermarket"},{"id":"o1","label":"Next to the bank"},{"id":"o2","label":"Inside the bank"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The pharmacy is next to the bank.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-places"},{"prompt":"Where should the listener meet the speaker?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At the supermarket"},{"id":"o1","label":"At the bank"},{"id":"o2","label":"At the pharmacy"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The speaker asks to meet at the pharmacy.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-places"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What is opposite the library?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The shop"},{"id":"o1","label":"The park"},{"id":"o2","label":"A cafe"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The library is opposite the shop.","passage":"Our street has a library, a small shop and a park. The library is opposite the shop. The park is behind the library. There are two benches in the park, but there is no cafe. My flat is next to the shop. I walk to the library because it is near my home.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is not in the park?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Two benches"},{"id":"o1","label":"A cafe"},{"id":"o2","label":"A path to the library"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The text explicitly says there is no cafe.","passage":"Our street has a library, a small shop and a park. The library is opposite the shop. The park is behind the library. There are two benches in the park, but there is no cafe. My flat is next to the shop. I walk to the library because it is near my home.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose a helpful direction.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It opens on Tuesday."},{"id":"o1","label":"It is opposite the shop."},{"id":"o2","label":"I like reading books."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Opposite the shop gives the requested location.","passage":"Visitor: Excuse me, where is the library? You know it is across the street from the shop.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use there is for one thing and there are for more than one. Next to means beside. Opposite means across from. Between needs two reference points. A location tells us where something is; a direction tells us how to get there.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a1-food","level":"A1","title":"Food and simple requests","objective":"Order food politely and distinguish countable and uncountable nouns.","project_prompt":"Write 40–80 words about yourself or a familiar person, using the unit situation as a model. Include clear basic facts and simple linked sentences.\nTopic: Food and simple requests\nFocus: Order food politely and distinguish countable and uncountable nouns.\nSource context: At the market, Tom buys two apples and a loaf of bread. Each apple costs one pound. The bread costs two pounds. Tom has five pounds, so he has one pound left. He wants some water, but the water stall is closed. He drinks water when he gets home.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use a or an with one countable item: a sandwich, an apple. Water and rice are usually uncountable; say some water or a glass of water. I would like is a polite way to request something. A price per item is not the total price.","description":"Order food politely and distinguish countable and uncountable nouns. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"How much does Tom spend?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Two pounds"},{"id":"o1","label":"Four pounds"},{"id":"o2","label":"Five pounds"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Two apples cost two pounds and the bread costs two more.","passage":"At the market, Tom buys two apples and a loaf of bread. Each apple costs one pound. The bread costs two pounds. Tom has five pounds, so he has one pound left. He wants some water, but the water stall is closed. He drinks water when he gets home.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Why does he not buy water?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He does not like it."},{"id":"o1","label":"He has no money left."},{"id":"o2","label":"The stall is closed."}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"He still has one pound, but the water stall is closed.","passage":"At the market, Tom buys two apples and a loaf of bread. Each apple costs one pound. The bread costs two pounds. Tom has five pounds, so he has one pound left. He wants some water, but the water stall is closed. He drinks water when he gets home.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use a or an with one countable item: a sandwich, an apple. Water and rice are usually uncountable; say some water or a glass of water. I would like is a polite way to request something. A price per item is not the total price.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with a or an: I would like ___ apple.","solution":"an","explanation":"Apple begins with a vowel sound.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete with some or a: Can I have ___ water?","solution":"some","explanation":"Water is uncountable in this request.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use a or an with one countable item: a sandwich, an apple. Water and rice are usually uncountable; say some water or a glass of water. I would like is a polite way to request something. A price per item is not the total price.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the polite, clear order.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A sandwich and a glass of water, please."},{"id":"o1","label":"You eat my water."},{"id":"o2","label":"Sandwiches are next to the bank."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The request names both items and includes please.","passage":"Server: What would you like? You want one sandwich and a glass of water, politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use a or an with one countable item: a sandwich, an apple. Water and rice are usually uncountable; say some water or a glass of water. I would like is a polite way to request something. A price per item is not the total price.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What drink is ordered?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Water"},{"id":"o1","label":"Coffee"},{"id":"o2","label":"Tea"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The customer orders a cup of tea.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-food"},{"prompt":"Where will the customer eat the sandwich?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Away from the cafe"},{"id":"o1","label":"At the table"},{"id":"o2","label":"In the kitchen"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Take away means the sandwich will not be eaten in the cafe.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-food"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"How much does Tom spend?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Two pounds"},{"id":"o1","label":"Four pounds"},{"id":"o2","label":"Five pounds"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Two apples cost two pounds and the bread costs two more.","passage":"At the market, Tom buys two apples and a loaf of bread. Each apple costs one pound. The bread costs two pounds. Tom has five pounds, so he has one pound left. He wants some water, but the water stall is closed. He drinks water when he gets home.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Why does he not buy water?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He does not like it."},{"id":"o1","label":"He has no money left."},{"id":"o2","label":"The stall is closed."}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"He still has one pound, but the water stall is closed.","passage":"At the market, Tom buys two apples and a loaf of bread. Each apple costs one pound. The bread costs two pounds. Tom has five pounds, so he has one pound left. He wants some water, but the water stall is closed. He drinks water when he gets home.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the polite, clear order.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A sandwich and a glass of water, please."},{"id":"o1","label":"You eat my water."},{"id":"o2","label":"Sandwiches are next to the bank."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The request names both items and includes please.","passage":"Server: What would you like? You want one sandwich and a glass of water, politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use a or an with one countable item: a sandwich, an apple. Water and rice are usually uncountable; say some water or a glass of water. I would like is a polite way to request something. A price per item is not the total price.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a1-abilities","level":"A1","title":"Interests and abilities","objective":"Describe preferences and abilities using like and can.","project_prompt":"Write 40–80 words about yourself or a familiar person, using the unit situation as a model. Include clear basic facts and simple linked sentences.\nTopic: Interests and abilities\nFocus: Describe preferences and abilities using like and can.\nSource context: Amir likes music. He can play the guitar, but he cannot play the piano. His sister can play the piano. On Fridays, they play music together at home. Amir also likes swimming, but he does not swim in winter. His sister does not like swimming. She likes drawing pictures.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Can describes ability: I can swim. The next verb stays in its base form: she can swim, not she can swims. Like describes enjoyment, not ability. I like playing tennis does not necessarily mean I play well. Use but to contrast two facts.","description":"Describe preferences and abilities using like and can. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which instrument can Amir play?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The piano"},{"id":"o1","label":"The guitar"},{"id":"o2","label":"Both instruments"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Amir can play the guitar but cannot play the piano.","passage":"Amir likes music. He can play the guitar, but he cannot play the piano. His sister can play the piano. On Fridays, they play music together at home. Amir also likes swimming, but he does not swim in winter. His sister does not like swimming. She likes drawing pictures.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What does his sister enjoy?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Drawing"},{"id":"o1","label":"Swimming"},{"id":"o2","label":"Winter swimming"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The sister likes drawing and does not like swimming.","passage":"Amir likes music. He can play the guitar, but he cannot play the piano. His sister can play the piano. On Fridays, they play music together at home. Amir also likes swimming, but he does not swim in winter. His sister does not like swimming. She likes drawing pictures.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Can describes ability: I can swim. The next verb stays in its base form: she can swim, not she can swims. Like describes enjoyment, not ability. I like playing tennis does not necessarily mean I play well. Use but to contrast two facts.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Correct the verb after can: She can plays the piano.","solution":"She can play the piano.","explanation":"Can is followed by the base form play.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete with can or cannot: I do not know how to swim. I ___ swim.","solution":"cannot","explanation":"Cannot expresses a lack of ability.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Can describes ability: I can swim. The next verb stays in its base form: she can swim, not she can swims. Like describes enjoyment, not ability. I like playing tennis does not necessarily mean I play well. Use but to contrast two facts.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the accurate reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"I cannot like Friday."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, but only on Friday."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, I can play very well."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply confirms ability while stating availability.","passage":"A club organiser asks: Can you play the guitar for our group? You can play, but you are only free on Friday.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Can describes ability: I can swim. The next verb stays in its base form: she can swim, not she can swims. Like describes enjoyment, not ability. I like playing tennis does not necessarily mean I play well. Use but to contrast two facts.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Who can cycle fifty kilometres?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The speaker"},{"id":"o1","label":"Jo"},{"id":"o2","label":"Both friends"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker assigns that ability to Jo.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-abilities"},{"prompt":"What do they do together on Sundays?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Cycle fifty kilometres"},{"id":"o1","label":"Swim"},{"id":"o2","label":"Walk"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"They walk together because both enjoy walking.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-abilities"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Which instrument can Amir play?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The piano"},{"id":"o1","label":"The guitar"},{"id":"o2","label":"Both instruments"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Amir can play the guitar but cannot play the piano.","passage":"Amir likes music. He can play the guitar, but he cannot play the piano. His sister can play the piano. On Fridays, they play music together at home. Amir also likes swimming, but he does not swim in winter. His sister does not like swimming. She likes drawing pictures.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What does his sister enjoy?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Drawing"},{"id":"o1","label":"Swimming"},{"id":"o2","label":"Winter swimming"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The sister likes drawing and does not like swimming.","passage":"Amir likes music. He can play the guitar, but he cannot play the piano. His sister can play the piano. On Fridays, they play music together at home. Amir also likes swimming, but he does not swim in winter. His sister does not like swimming. She likes drawing pictures.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the accurate reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"I cannot like Friday."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, but only on Friday."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, I can play very well."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply confirms ability while stating availability.","passage":"A club organiser asks: Can you play the guitar for our group? You can play, but you are only free on Friday.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Can describes ability: I can swim. The next verb stays in its base form: she can swim, not she can swims. Like describes enjoyment, not ability. I like playing tennis does not necessarily mean I play well. Use but to contrast two facts.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a1-plans","level":"A1","title":"Shopping and near-future plans","objective":"Understand prices, demonstratives and simple plans with going to.","project_prompt":"Write 40–80 words about yourself or a familiar person, using the unit situation as a model. Include clear basic facts and simple linked sentences.\nTopic: Shopping and near-future plans\nFocus: Understand prices, demonstratives and simple plans with going to.\nSource context: Lily is going to visit her aunt tomorrow. Today, she is buying a present. She sees a blue scarf for ten pounds and a green scarf for eight pounds. Her aunt likes green, so Lily buys the green scarf. She is going to travel by bus. The bus stop is near her house.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: This and that refer to one item; these and those refer to several. This is near the speaker; that is farther away. Use am, is or are plus going to and a base verb for a plan: we are going to visit. A plan is an intention, not proof it has already happened.","description":"Understand prices, demonstratives and simple plans with going to. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why does Lily choose the green scarf?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Her aunt likes green."},{"id":"o1","label":"There is no blue scarf."},{"id":"o2","label":"It costs ten pounds."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Her aunt's colour preference is the stated reason.","passage":"Lily is going to visit her aunt tomorrow. Today, she is buying a present. She sees a blue scarf for ten pounds and a green scarf for eight pounds. Her aunt likes green, so Lily buys the green scarf. She is going to travel by bus. The bus stop is near her house.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How is Lily planning to travel?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"By train"},{"id":"o1","label":"On foot"},{"id":"o2","label":"By bus"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She is going to travel by bus; the trip is tomorrow.","passage":"Lily is going to visit her aunt tomorrow. Today, she is buying a present. She sees a blue scarf for ten pounds and a green scarf for eight pounds. Her aunt likes green, so Lily buys the green scarf. She is going to travel by bus. The bus stop is near her house.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"This and that refer to one item; these and those refer to several. This is near the speaker; that is farther away. Use am, is or are plus going to and a base verb for a plan: we are going to visit. A plan is an intention, not proof it has already happened.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with this or these: ___ shoes are blue.","solution":"These","explanation":"Shoes is plural, so use these.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: We ___ going to visit our aunt.","solution":"are","explanation":"We takes are before going to.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"This and that refer to one item; these and those refer to several. This is near the speaker; that is farther away. Use am, is or are plus going to and a base verb for a plan: we are going to visit. A plan is an intention, not proof it has already happened.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose your reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The green one, please."},{"id":"o1","label":"I am blue tomorrow."},{"id":"o2","label":"They travel at eight."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The green one clearly refers back to the green scarf.","passage":"Shop assistant: Would you like the blue scarf or the green one? You have decided on the green scarf.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"This and that refer to one item; these and those refer to several. This is near the speaker; that is farther away. Use am, is or are plus going to and a base verb for a plan: we are going to visit. A plan is an intention, not proof it has already happened.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What should a student bring?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A scarf"},{"id":"o1","label":"A student card"},{"id":"o2","label":"A bus ticket"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"A student card is requested for the student ticket.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-plans"},{"prompt":"Where is the meeting?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Outside the entrance"},{"id":"o1","label":"Inside the shop"},{"id":"o2","label":"At the bus station"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The group meets at ten outside the entrance.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a1-plans"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why does Lily choose the green scarf?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Her aunt likes green."},{"id":"o1","label":"There is no blue scarf."},{"id":"o2","label":"It costs ten pounds."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Her aunt's colour preference is the stated reason.","passage":"Lily is going to visit her aunt tomorrow. Today, she is buying a present. She sees a blue scarf for ten pounds and a green scarf for eight pounds. Her aunt likes green, so Lily buys the green scarf. She is going to travel by bus. The bus stop is near her house.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How is Lily planning to travel?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"By train"},{"id":"o1","label":"On foot"},{"id":"o2","label":"By bus"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She is going to travel by bus; the trip is tomorrow.","passage":"Lily is going to visit her aunt tomorrow. Today, she is buying a present. She sees a blue scarf for ten pounds and a green scarf for eight pounds. Her aunt likes green, so Lily buys the green scarf. She is going to travel by bus. The bus stop is near her house.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose your reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The green one, please."},{"id":"o1","label":"I am blue tomorrow."},{"id":"o2","label":"They travel at eight."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The green one clearly refers back to the green scarf.","passage":"Shop assistant: Would you like the blue scarf or the green one? You have decided on the green scarf.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"This and that refer to one item; these and those refer to several. This is near the speaker; that is farther away. Use am, is or are plus going to and a base verb for a plan: we are going to visit. A plan is an intention, not proof it has already happened.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a2-past","level":"A2","title":"Past events and short narratives","objective":"Sequence finished events using past-simple verbs and time markers.","project_prompt":"Write 80–120 words about a comparable everyday situation. Describe events or plans, give a reason, and include a practical request or response.\nTopic: Past events and short narratives\nFocus: Sequence finished events using past-simple verbs and time markers.\nSource context: Last Sunday, Ella planned to visit a museum. First, she checked the opening times online. Then she caught a bus, but she left her wallet at home. She got off at the next stop and walked back. Finally, she visited the museum in the afternoon. She missed the morning tour, but she enjoyed the exhibition.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use the past simple for finished events: visited, went, bought. After did or did not, use the base verb. First, then and finally organise events. Yesterday and last weekend refer to finished time. Distinguish an event from the reason for it.","description":"Sequence finished events using past-simple verbs and time markers. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did Ella go home?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The museum was closed."},{"id":"o1","label":"She forgot her wallet."},{"id":"o2","label":"She disliked the bus."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She returned because she had left her wallet at home.","passage":"Last Sunday, Ella planned to visit a museum. First, she checked the opening times online. Then she caught a bus, but she left her wallet at home. She got off at the next stop and walked back. Finally, she visited the museum in the afternoon. She missed the morning tour, but she enjoyed the exhibition.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What did she miss?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The whole exhibition"},{"id":"o1","label":"The afternoon bus"},{"id":"o2","label":"The morning tour"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She eventually visited, but missed the morning tour.","passage":"Last Sunday, Ella planned to visit a museum. First, she checked the opening times online. Then she caught a bus, but she left her wallet at home. She got off at the next stop and walked back. Finally, she visited the museum in the afternoon. She missed the morning tour, but she enjoyed the exhibition.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the past simple for finished events: visited, went, bought. After did or did not, use the base verb. First, then and finally organise events. Yesterday and last weekend refer to finished time. Distinguish an event from the reason for it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Correct: She did not visited the museum in the morning.","solution":"She did not visit the museum in the morning.","explanation":"Did not requires the base form visit.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete with the past of buy: I ___ a ticket yesterday.","solution":"bought","explanation":"Bought is the irregular past of buy.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the past simple for finished events: visited, went, bought. After did or did not, use the base verb. First, then and finally organise events. Yesterday and last weekend refer to finished time. Distinguish an event from the reason for it.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the relevant response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Yes, but I missed the tour."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, I go tomorrow."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, the wallet is blue."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply answers the past-tense question and adds the relevant contrast.","passage":"Friend: Did you enjoy the museum yesterday? You enjoyed it, although you missed the tour.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the past simple for finished events: visited, went, bought. After did or did not, use the base verb. First, then and finally organise events. Yesterday and last weekend refer to finished time. Distinguish an event from the reason for it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did they take a bus?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It was cheaper."},{"id":"o1","label":"The train was cancelled."},{"id":"o2","label":"They missed their stop."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The cancellation caused the change of transport.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-past"},{"prompt":"What did they do before the beach?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"They ate lunch."},{"id":"o1","label":"They bought a train."},{"id":"o2","label":"They went home."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Lunch came before going to the beach.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-past"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did Ella go home?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The museum was closed."},{"id":"o1","label":"She forgot her wallet."},{"id":"o2","label":"She disliked the bus."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She returned because she had left her wallet at home.","passage":"Last Sunday, Ella planned to visit a museum. First, she checked the opening times online. Then she caught a bus, but she left her wallet at home. She got off at the next stop and walked back. Finally, she visited the museum in the afternoon. She missed the morning tour, but she enjoyed the exhibition.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What did she miss?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The whole exhibition"},{"id":"o1","label":"The afternoon bus"},{"id":"o2","label":"The morning tour"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She eventually visited, but missed the morning tour.","passage":"Last Sunday, Ella planned to visit a museum. First, she checked the opening times online. Then she caught a bus, but she left her wallet at home. She got off at the next stop and walked back. Finally, she visited the museum in the afternoon. She missed the morning tour, but she enjoyed the exhibition.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the relevant response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Yes, but I missed the tour."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, I go tomorrow."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, the wallet is blue."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply answers the past-tense question and adds the relevant contrast.","passage":"Friend: Did you enjoy the museum yesterday? You enjoyed it, although you missed the tour.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use the past simple for finished events: visited, went, bought. After did or did not, use the base verb. First, then and finally organise events. Yesterday and last weekend refer to finished time. Distinguish an event from the reason for it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a2-travel","level":"A2","title":"Travel and arrangements","objective":"Handle straightforward travel problems and understand arrangements.","project_prompt":"Write 80–120 words about a comparable everyday situation. Describe events or plans, give a reason, and include a practical request or response.\nTopic: Travel and arrangements\nFocus: Handle straightforward travel problems and understand arrangements.\nSource context: Kai is visiting Bristol next week. He is staying with his cousin, so he does not need a hotel. His train leaves at nine, but he plans to reach the station at eight thirty. He bought a return ticket because he is coming home on Sunday. His cousin is meeting him at the station, not at the house.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use the present continuous for a personal arrangement: I am meeting Ana tomorrow. A timetable may use the present simple: the train leaves at six. Could you...? makes a polite request. Single means one way; return includes travel back.","description":"Handle straightforward travel problems and understand arrangements. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why does Kai not book a hotel?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He is staying with his cousin."},{"id":"o1","label":"He is not travelling."},{"id":"o2","label":"He is sleeping on the train."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"His cousin is providing accommodation.","passage":"Kai is visiting Bristol next week. He is staying with his cousin, so he does not need a hotel. His train leaves at nine, but he plans to reach the station at eight thirty. He bought a return ticket because he is coming home on Sunday. His cousin is meeting him at the station, not at the house.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Where will they first meet?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At the house"},{"id":"o1","label":"At the station"},{"id":"o2","label":"At a hotel"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"His cousin is meeting him at the station.","passage":"Kai is visiting Bristol next week. He is staying with his cousin, so he does not need a hotel. His train leaves at nine, but he plans to reach the station at eight thirty. He bought a return ticket because he is coming home on Sunday. His cousin is meeting him at the station, not at the house.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the present continuous for a personal arrangement: I am meeting Ana tomorrow. A timetable may use the present simple: the train leaves at six. Could you...? makes a polite request. Single means one way; return includes travel back.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: I ___ meeting my cousin tomorrow.","solution":"am","explanation":"I takes am in the present continuous.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete with single or return: I need to travel there and back. I need a ___ ticket.","solution":"return","explanation":"A return ticket covers both directions.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the present continuous for a personal arrangement: I am meeting Ana tomorrow. A timetable may use the present simple: the train leaves at six. Could you...? makes a polite request. Single means one way; return includes travel back.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which request fits?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"You never run any trains."},{"id":"o1","label":"Could you tell me which platform my train leaves from?"},{"id":"o2","label":"I already know the platform."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The request is polite and specifies the information needed.","passage":"At a station, your train has disappeared from the departure board. You want to ask an employee for help politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use the present continuous for a personal arrangement: I am meeting Ana tomorrow. A timetable may use the present simple: the train leaves at six. Could you...? makes a polite request. Single means one way; return includes travel back.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Which platform is for York?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Two"},{"id":"o1","label":"Six"},{"id":"o2","label":"Four"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The announcement changes the York platform to four.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-travel"},{"prompt":"When does the Leeds service leave?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Six fifteen"},{"id":"o1","label":"Six thirty"},{"id":"o2","label":"Four thirty"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The Leeds service is at six thirty.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-travel"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why does Kai not book a hotel?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He is staying with his cousin."},{"id":"o1","label":"He is not travelling."},{"id":"o2","label":"He is sleeping on the train."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"His cousin is providing accommodation.","passage":"Kai is visiting Bristol next week. He is staying with his cousin, so he does not need a hotel. His train leaves at nine, but he plans to reach the station at eight thirty. He bought a return ticket because he is coming home on Sunday. His cousin is meeting him at the station, not at the house.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Where will they first meet?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At the house"},{"id":"o1","label":"At the station"},{"id":"o2","label":"At a hotel"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"His cousin is meeting him at the station.","passage":"Kai is visiting Bristol next week. He is staying with his cousin, so he does not need a hotel. His train leaves at nine, but he plans to reach the station at eight thirty. He bought a return ticket because he is coming home on Sunday. His cousin is meeting him at the station, not at the house.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which request fits?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"You never run any trains."},{"id":"o1","label":"Could you tell me which platform my train leaves from?"},{"id":"o2","label":"I already know the platform."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The request is polite and specifies the information needed.","passage":"At a station, your train has disappeared from the departure board. You want to ask an employee for help politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use the present continuous for a personal arrangement: I am meeting Ana tomorrow. A timetable may use the present simple: the train leaves at six. Could you...? makes a polite request. Single means one way; return includes travel back.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a2-comparisons","level":"A2","title":"Choices and comparisons","objective":"Compare alternatives using comparatives, superlatives and enough.","project_prompt":"Write 80–120 words about a comparable everyday situation. Describe events or plans, give a reason, and include a practical request or response.\nTopic: Choices and comparisons\nFocus: Compare alternatives using comparatives, superlatives and enough.\nSource context: Maya needs a laptop for writing essays. Model A is cheaper than Model B, but its battery lasts only three hours. Model B lasts eight hours and is lighter. Maya often studies where there are no sockets. She chooses Model B even though it costs more. She does not need the fastest machine; she needs one that works all day.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Short adjectives often take -er: cheaper. Longer adjectives usually use more: more comfortable. Use than to introduce the comparison. The cheapest identifies the lowest price in a group. Cheap enough means the price meets a need, not necessarily that it is the lowest.","description":"Compare alternatives using comparatives, superlatives and enough. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What matters most to Maya?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The lowest price"},{"id":"o1","label":"A long battery life"},{"id":"o2","label":"The fastest processor"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She needs to work away from sockets for much of the day.","passage":"Maya needs a laptop for writing essays. Model A is cheaper than Model B, but its battery lasts only three hours. Model B lasts eight hours and is lighter. Maya often studies where there are no sockets. She chooses Model B even though it costs more. She does not need the fastest machine; she needs one that works all day.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which statement is supported?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Model B is lighter."},{"id":"o1","label":"Model A lasts eight hours."},{"id":"o2","label":"Maya buys both models."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The text explicitly describes Model B as lighter.","passage":"Maya needs a laptop for writing essays. Model A is cheaper than Model B, but its battery lasts only three hours. Model B lasts eight hours and is lighter. Maya often studies where there are no sockets. She chooses Model B even though it costs more. She does not need the fastest machine; she needs one that works all day.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Short adjectives often take -er: cheaper. Longer adjectives usually use more: more comfortable. Use than to introduce the comparison. The cheapest identifies the lowest price in a group. Cheap enough means the price meets a need, not necessarily that it is the lowest.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the comparative: This bag is ___ (light) than that one.","solution":"lighter","explanation":"Light takes -er.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Correct: This chair is more comfortabler than mine.","solution":"This chair is more comfortable than mine.","explanation":"Use more comfortable, not a double comparative.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Short adjectives often take -er: cheaper. Longer adjectives usually use more: more comfortable. Use than to introduce the comparison. The cheapest identifies the lowest price in a group. Cheap enough means the price meets a need, not necessarily that it is the lowest.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the useful follow-up.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"How long does the battery last?"},{"id":"o1","label":"Cheap means it is always best."},{"id":"o2","label":"I do not use a laptop."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The question checks the feature relevant to the decision.","passage":"Assistant: This model is the cheapest. You need to check its battery before deciding.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Short adjectives often take -er: cheaper. Longer adjectives usually use more: more comfortable. Use than to introduce the comparison. The cheapest identifies the lowest price in a group. Cheap enough means the price meets a need, not necessarily that it is the lowest.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"How much is the red backpack?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Fifteen pounds"},{"id":"o1","label":"Twenty pounds"},{"id":"o2","label":"Twenty-five pounds"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"It costs five pounds more than twenty.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-comparisons"},{"prompt":"Why is the red one chosen?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It holds more."},{"id":"o1","label":"It is waterproof."},{"id":"o2","label":"It is cheaper."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Waterproofing matters when cycling in rain.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-comparisons"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What matters most to Maya?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The lowest price"},{"id":"o1","label":"A long battery life"},{"id":"o2","label":"The fastest processor"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She needs to work away from sockets for much of the day.","passage":"Maya needs a laptop for writing essays. Model A is cheaper than Model B, but its battery lasts only three hours. Model B lasts eight hours and is lighter. Maya often studies where there are no sockets. She chooses Model B even though it costs more. She does not need the fastest machine; she needs one that works all day.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which statement is supported?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Model B is lighter."},{"id":"o1","label":"Model A lasts eight hours."},{"id":"o2","label":"Maya buys both models."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The text explicitly describes Model B as lighter.","passage":"Maya needs a laptop for writing essays. Model A is cheaper than Model B, but its battery lasts only three hours. Model B lasts eight hours and is lighter. Maya often studies where there are no sockets. She chooses Model B even though it costs more. She does not need the fastest machine; she needs one that works all day.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the useful follow-up.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"How long does the battery last?"},{"id":"o1","label":"Cheap means it is always best."},{"id":"o2","label":"I do not use a laptop."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The question checks the feature relevant to the decision.","passage":"Assistant: This model is the cheapest. You need to check its battery before deciding.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Short adjectives often take -er: cheaper. Longer adjectives usually use more: more comfortable. Use than to introduce the comparison. The cheapest identifies the lowest price in a group. Cheap enough means the price meets a need, not necessarily that it is the lowest.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a2-health","level":"A2","title":"Health, advice and obligations","objective":"Distinguish advice, rules and absence of obligation.","project_prompt":"Write 80–120 words about a comparable everyday situation. Describe events or plans, give a reason, and include a practical request or response.\nTopic: Health, advice and obligations\nFocus: Distinguish advice, rules and absence of obligation.\nSource context: Ben has a sore throat and feels tired. He calls his clinic before going there. The receptionist says he should drink water and rest while he waits for his appointment. He must bring his identification card. He does not have to arrive an hour early; ten minutes is enough. The appointment is at eleven.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Should gives advice. Must or have to expresses an obligation. Must not prohibits an action; do not have to means it is optional. These meanings are different. Describe a symptom clearly, but seek professional advice for real health decisions.","description":"Distinguish advice, rules and absence of obligation. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What must Ben bring?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A bottle of medicine"},{"id":"o1","label":"An identification card"},{"id":"o2","label":"An extra ticket"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Bringing identification is the stated requirement.","passage":"Ben has a sore throat and feels tired. He calls his clinic before going there. The receptionist says he should drink water and rest while he waits for his appointment. He must bring his identification card. He does not have to arrive an hour early; ten minutes is enough. The appointment is at eleven.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is optional or unnecessary?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Calling the clinic"},{"id":"o1","label":"Resting"},{"id":"o2","label":"Arriving an hour early"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"He does not have to arrive an hour early.","passage":"Ben has a sore throat and feels tired. He calls his clinic before going there. The receptionist says he should drink water and rest while he waits for his appointment. He must bring his identification card. He does not have to arrive an hour early; ten minutes is enough. The appointment is at eleven.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Should gives advice. Must or have to expresses an obligation. Must not prohibits an action; do not have to means it is optional. These meanings are different. Describe a symptom clearly, but seek professional advice for real health decisions.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with should or must not: For advice, say: You ___ rest.","solution":"should","explanation":"Should signals advice, not a prohibition.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Replace must not with the correct phrase for no obligation: You ___ book in advance; walk-ins are welcome.","solution":"do not have to","explanation":"No obligation is different from a ban on booking.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Should gives advice. Must or have to expresses an obligation. Must not prohibits an action; do not have to means it is optional. These meanings are different. Describe a symptom clearly, but seek professional advice for real health decisions.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose a clear reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Eleven, please. I work in the afternoon."},{"id":"o1","label":"Three is earlier than eleven."},{"id":"o2","label":"I must not have an appointment."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply selects the appointment that fits the schedule.","passage":"Receptionist: We can see you at eleven or at three. You are working all afternoon and want the earlier appointment.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Should gives advice. Must or have to expresses an obligation. Must not prohibits an action; do not have to means it is optional. These meanings are different. Describe a symptom clearly, but seek professional advice for real health decisions.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What is not required?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Bringing the letter"},{"id":"o1","label":"Fasting"},{"id":"o2","label":"Calling if unable to attend"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker explicitly says fasting is not needed.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-health"},{"prompt":"When should a cancellation be reported?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"After Monday"},{"id":"o1","label":"On Monday evening"},{"id":"o2","label":"Before Friday"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The instruction is to phone before Friday.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-health"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What must Ben bring?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A bottle of medicine"},{"id":"o1","label":"An identification card"},{"id":"o2","label":"An extra ticket"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Bringing identification is the stated requirement.","passage":"Ben has a sore throat and feels tired. He calls his clinic before going there. The receptionist says he should drink water and rest while he waits for his appointment. He must bring his identification card. He does not have to arrive an hour early; ten minutes is enough. The appointment is at eleven.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is optional or unnecessary?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Calling the clinic"},{"id":"o1","label":"Resting"},{"id":"o2","label":"Arriving an hour early"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"He does not have to arrive an hour early.","passage":"Ben has a sore throat and feels tired. He calls his clinic before going there. The receptionist says he should drink water and rest while he waits for his appointment. He must bring his identification card. He does not have to arrive an hour early; ten minutes is enough. The appointment is at eleven.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose a clear reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Eleven, please. I work in the afternoon."},{"id":"o1","label":"Three is earlier than eleven."},{"id":"o2","label":"I must not have an appointment."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply selects the appointment that fits the schedule.","passage":"Receptionist: We can see you at eleven or at three. You are working all afternoon and want the earlier appointment.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Should gives advice. Must or have to expresses an obligation. Must not prohibits an action; do not have to means it is optional. These meanings are different. Describe a symptom clearly, but seek professional advice for real health decisions.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a2-experiences","level":"A2","title":"Experiences and unfinished time","objective":"Distinguish present-perfect experience from finished past events.","project_prompt":"Write 80–120 words about a comparable everyday situation. Describe events or plans, give a reason, and include a practical request or response.\nTopic: Experiences and unfinished time\nFocus: Distinguish present-perfect experience from finished past events.\nSource context: Sofia has visited Scotland twice. Her first visit was in 2022, when she stayed in Edinburgh. This year she has travelled less because she is saving money. She has never been to Wales, but she hopes to go next spring. Her brother has gone to Cardiff for a course and will return on Friday.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use have or has plus a past participle for experience without a finished time: I have visited York. Use the past simple with yesterday or last year. Ever asks about experience; never says it has not happened. Been to usually describes a completed visit; gone to may mean the person is still away.","description":"Distinguish present-perfect experience from finished past events. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which place has Sofia not visited?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Scotland"},{"id":"o1","label":"Edinburgh"},{"id":"o2","label":"Wales"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She has never been to Wales.","passage":"Sofia has visited Scotland twice. Her first visit was in 2022, when she stayed in Edinburgh. This year she has travelled less because she is saving money. She has never been to Wales, but she hopes to go next spring. Her brother has gone to Cardiff for a course and will return on Friday.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What can we infer about her brother?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He is away on a course."},{"id":"o1","label":"He has never travelled."},{"id":"o2","label":"He returned yesterday."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Has gone to Cardiff and will return on Friday indicate he is still away.","passage":"Sofia has visited Scotland twice. Her first visit was in 2022, when she stayed in Edinburgh. This year she has travelled less because she is saving money. She has never been to Wales, but she hopes to go next spring. Her brother has gone to Cardiff for a course and will return on Friday.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use have or has plus a past participle for experience without a finished time: I have visited York. Use the past simple with yesterday or last year. Ever asks about experience; never says it has not happened. Been to usually describes a completed visit; gone to may mean the person is still away.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: She has never ___ (be) to Wales.","solution":"been","explanation":"Been is the past participle of be.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Correct the tense with a finished time: I have visited York yesterday.","solution":"I visited York yesterday.","explanation":"Yesterday requires the past simple here.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use have or has plus a past participle for experience without a finished time: I have visited York. Use the past simple with yesterday or last year. Ever asks about experience; never says it has not happened. Been to usually describes a completed visit; gone to may mean the person is still away.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the appropriate answer.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Yes, I went there last year."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, I go there yesterday."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, I have gone there every Monday."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Answer the experience question and use past simple for last year.","passage":"Friend: Have you ever been to Scotland? You went there once, last year.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use have or has plus a past participle for experience without a finished time: I have visited York. Use the past simple with yesterday or last year. Ever asks about experience; never says it has not happened. Been to usually describes a completed visit; gone to may mean the person is still away.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What has the speaker tried?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Snowboarding"},{"id":"o1","label":"Skiing"},{"id":"o2","label":"Neither sport"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker has tried skiing once.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-experiences"},{"prompt":"When was that experience?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Three years ago"},{"id":"o1","label":"Last winter"},{"id":"o2","label":"Next winter"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Three years ago specifies the finished time.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-experiences"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Which place has Sofia not visited?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Scotland"},{"id":"o1","label":"Edinburgh"},{"id":"o2","label":"Wales"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She has never been to Wales.","passage":"Sofia has visited Scotland twice. Her first visit was in 2022, when she stayed in Edinburgh. This year she has travelled less because she is saving money. She has never been to Wales, but she hopes to go next spring. Her brother has gone to Cardiff for a course and will return on Friday.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What can we infer about her brother?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He is away on a course."},{"id":"o1","label":"He has never travelled."},{"id":"o2","label":"He returned yesterday."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Has gone to Cardiff and will return on Friday indicate he is still away.","passage":"Sofia has visited Scotland twice. Her first visit was in 2022, when she stayed in Edinburgh. This year she has travelled less because she is saving money. She has never been to Wales, but she hopes to go next spring. Her brother has gone to Cardiff for a course and will return on Friday.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the appropriate answer.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Yes, I went there last year."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, I go there yesterday."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, I have gone there every Monday."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Answer the experience question and use past simple for last year.","passage":"Friend: Have you ever been to Scotland? You went there once, last year.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use have or has plus a past participle for experience without a finished time: I have visited York. Use the past simple with yesterday or last year. Ever asks about experience; never says it has not happened. Been to usually describes a completed visit; gone to may mean the person is still away.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"a2-future","level":"A2","title":"Plans, predictions and invitations","objective":"Discuss intentions and likely results using future forms and first conditionals.","project_prompt":"Write 80–120 words about a comparable everyday situation. Describe events or plans, give a reason, and include a practical request or response.\nTopic: Plans, predictions and invitations\nFocus: Discuss intentions and likely results using future forms and first conditionals.\nSource context: Our class is organising a picnic for Saturday. We are going to bring food to share. If it rains, we will meet in the community hall instead. The hall is already booked, so we will not need to cancel. Mina cannot come because she is visiting her grandparents. She offers to help prepare sandwiches on Friday.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Going to often describes an existing intention. Will can express a prediction or a decision made now. In a first conditional, use if plus present simple, then will plus base verb: If it rains, we will stay inside. An invitation should be accepted or declined clearly and politely.","description":"Discuss intentions and likely results using future forms and first conditionals. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What will happen if it rains?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The event will be cancelled."},{"id":"o1","label":"They will meet in the hall."},{"id":"o2","label":"They will go on Sunday."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"A booked indoor venue provides the alternative.","passage":"Our class is organising a picnic for Saturday. We are going to bring food to share. If it rains, we will meet in the community hall instead. The hall is already booked, so we will not need to cancel. Mina cannot come because she is visiting her grandparents. She offers to help prepare sandwiches on Friday.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How will Mina contribute?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"By booking the hall"},{"id":"o1","label":"By attending on Saturday"},{"id":"o2","label":"By preparing sandwiches on Friday"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She cannot attend but offers help the previous day.","passage":"Our class is organising a picnic for Saturday. We are going to bring food to share. If it rains, we will meet in the community hall instead. The hall is already booked, so we will not need to cancel. Mina cannot come because she is visiting her grandparents. She offers to help prepare sandwiches on Friday.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Going to often describes an existing intention. Will can express a prediction or a decision made now. In a first conditional, use if plus present simple, then will plus base verb: If it rains, we will stay inside. An invitation should be accepted or declined clearly and politely.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: If it ___ (rain), we will use the hall.","solution":"rains","explanation":"Use present simple in the if-clause.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Correct: If she will arrive early, we will start at ten.","solution":"If she arrives early, we will start at ten.","explanation":"Do not use will in this ordinary first-conditional if-clause.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Going to often describes an existing intention. Will can express a prediction or a decision made now. In a first conditional, use if plus present simple, then will plus base verb: If it rains, we will stay inside. An invitation should be accepted or declined clearly and politely.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose a polite refusal.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Thanks for inviting me, but I cannot make it."},{"id":"o1","label":"You must cancel the picnic."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, nobody likes picnics."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply appreciates the invitation and states the limitation without attacking the event.","passage":"Friend: Would you like to join our picnic? You cannot attend, but you want to thank them.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Going to often describes an existing intention. Will can express a prediction or a decision made now. In a first conditional, use if plus present simple, then will plus base verb: If it rains, we will stay inside. An invitation should be accepted or declined clearly and politely.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Where should people meet?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"At the park"},{"id":"o1","label":"At the school gate"},{"id":"o2","label":"At home"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker explicitly requests the school gate.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-future"},{"prompt":"What will the speaker do if the bus is late?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Cancel without notice"},{"id":"o1","label":"Leave alone"},{"id":"o2","label":"Send a message"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"A message will communicate the changed situation.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_a2-future"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What will happen if it rains?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The event will be cancelled."},{"id":"o1","label":"They will meet in the hall."},{"id":"o2","label":"They will go on Sunday."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"A booked indoor venue provides the alternative.","passage":"Our class is organising a picnic for Saturday. We are going to bring food to share. If it rains, we will meet in the community hall instead. The hall is already booked, so we will not need to cancel. Mina cannot come because she is visiting her grandparents. She offers to help prepare sandwiches on Friday.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How will Mina contribute?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"By booking the hall"},{"id":"o1","label":"By attending on Saturday"},{"id":"o2","label":"By preparing sandwiches on Friday"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"She cannot attend but offers help the previous day.","passage":"Our class is organising a picnic for Saturday. We are going to bring food to share. If it rains, we will meet in the community hall instead. The hall is already booked, so we will not need to cancel. Mina cannot come because she is visiting her grandparents. She offers to help prepare sandwiches on Friday.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose a polite refusal.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Thanks for inviting me, but I cannot make it."},{"id":"o1","label":"You must cancel the picnic."},{"id":"o2","label":"No, nobody likes picnics."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply appreciates the invitation and states the limitation without attacking the event.","passage":"Friend: Would you like to join our picnic? You cannot attend, but you want to thank them.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Going to often describes an existing intention. Will can express a prediction or a decision made now. In a first conditional, use if plus present simple, then will plus base verb: If it rains, we will stay inside. An invitation should be accepted or declined clearly and politely.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b1-narratives","level":"B1","title":"Narratives and turning points","objective":"Connect background actions with completed events in a narrative.","project_prompt":"Write 120–180 words explaining a comparable problem, its background and a proposed response. Connect events and reasons; consider an alternative.\nTopic: Narratives and turning points\nFocus: Connect background actions with completed events in a narrative.\nSource context: While Joel was walking home, he noticed an elderly neighbour standing outside her flat. She had locked herself out and had left her phone inside. Joel initially offered to call a locksmith. Then she remembered that her daughter kept a spare key. He lent her his phone, and her daughter arrived twenty minutes later. Joel was late for dinner, but the problem was solved without damaging the door.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: The past continuous sets an action in progress; the past simple marks an event that interrupts it. The past perfect places an event before another past reference point. A clear narrative establishes context, identifies a turning point and explains the result. Do not infer a motive that the narrator does not provide.","description":"Connect background actions with completed events in a narrative. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What happened before Joel arrived?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The daughter broke the door."},{"id":"o1","label":"The neighbour locked herself out."},{"id":"o2","label":"Joel called a locksmith."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Had locked herself out places the event before his encounter.","passage":"While Joel was walking home, he noticed an elderly neighbour standing outside her flat. She had locked herself out and had left her phone inside. Joel initially offered to call a locksmith. Then she remembered that her daughter kept a spare key. He lent her his phone, and her daughter arrived twenty minutes later. Joel was late for dinner, but the problem was solved without damaging the door.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which action actually solved the problem?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Calling the daughter with a spare key"},{"id":"o1","label":"Breaking the lock"},{"id":"o2","label":"Waiting for a locksmith"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The daughter brought the spare key; a locksmith was only proposed.","passage":"While Joel was walking home, he noticed an elderly neighbour standing outside her flat. She had locked herself out and had left her phone inside. Joel initially offered to call a locksmith. Then she remembered that her daughter kept a spare key. He lent her his phone, and her daughter arrived twenty minutes later. Joel was late for dinner, but the problem was solved without damaging the door.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"The past continuous sets an action in progress; the past simple marks an event that interrupts it. The past perfect places an event before another past reference point. A clear narrative establishes context, identifies a turning point and explains the result. Do not infer a motive that the narrator does not provide.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the background action: While he ___ (walk) home, he noticed her.","solution":"was walking","explanation":"Past continuous supplies the ongoing background.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Use past perfect: She ___ (leave) her phone inside before he arrived.","solution":"had left","explanation":"Had left marks the earlier of the two past events.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"The past continuous sets an action in progress; the past simple marks an event that interrupts it. The past perfect places an event before another past reference point. A clear narrative establishes context, identifies a turning point and explains the result. Do not infer a motive that the narrator does not provide.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the balanced reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"You always cause trouble."},{"id":"o1","label":"I am glad we sorted it out; I can let my family know."},{"id":"o2","label":"Nothing happened and nobody helped."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply acknowledges the solution and manages the delay constructively.","passage":"Neighbour: I am sorry I made you late. You want to reassure her without pretending you had no other plans.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"The past continuous sets an action in progress; the past simple marks an event that interrupts it. The past perfect places an event before another past reference point. A clear narrative establishes context, identifies a turning point and explains the result. Do not infer a motive that the narrator does not provide.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did the speaker leave?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"To collect a parcel"},{"id":"o1","label":"Because of the fire alarm"},{"id":"o2","label":"To visit the driver"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The alarm triggered the departure.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-narratives"},{"prompt":"What happened to the parcel?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It was left inside."},{"id":"o1","label":"A neighbour signed for it."},{"id":"o2","label":"It returned to the depot."}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The driver took it back because the recipient was outside.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-narratives"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What happened before Joel arrived?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The daughter broke the door."},{"id":"o1","label":"The neighbour locked herself out."},{"id":"o2","label":"Joel called a locksmith."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Had locked herself out places the event before his encounter.","passage":"While Joel was walking home, he noticed an elderly neighbour standing outside her flat. She had locked herself out and had left her phone inside. Joel initially offered to call a locksmith. Then she remembered that her daughter kept a spare key. He lent her his phone, and her daughter arrived twenty minutes later. Joel was late for dinner, but the problem was solved without damaging the door.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which action actually solved the problem?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Calling the daughter with a spare key"},{"id":"o1","label":"Breaking the lock"},{"id":"o2","label":"Waiting for a locksmith"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The daughter brought the spare key; a locksmith was only proposed.","passage":"While Joel was walking home, he noticed an elderly neighbour standing outside her flat. She had locked herself out and had left her phone inside. Joel initially offered to call a locksmith. Then she remembered that her daughter kept a spare key. He lent her his phone, and her daughter arrived twenty minutes later. Joel was late for dinner, but the problem was solved without damaging the door.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the balanced reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"You always cause trouble."},{"id":"o1","label":"I am glad we sorted it out; I can let my family know."},{"id":"o2","label":"Nothing happened and nobody helped."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply acknowledges the solution and manages the delay constructively.","passage":"Neighbour: I am sorry I made you late. You want to reassure her without pretending you had no other plans.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"The past continuous sets an action in progress; the past simple marks an event that interrupts it. The past perfect places an event before another past reference point. A clear narrative establishes context, identifies a turning point and explains the result. Do not infer a motive that the narrator does not provide.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b1-learning","level":"B1","title":"Learning strategies and advice","objective":"Explain problems, recommend strategies and discuss hypothetical choices.","project_prompt":"Write 120–180 words explaining a comparable problem, its background and a proposed response. Connect events and reasons; consider an alternative.\nTopic: Learning strategies and advice\nFocus: Explain problems, recommend strategies and discuss hypothetical choices.\nSource context: Priya can understand articles when she reads slowly, but she forgets new words during conversation. Her tutor suggests keeping a small notebook with phrases rather than isolated translations. Priya also records a short summary after reading and listens to it the next day. The tutor does not promise instant fluency. The aim is to practise retrieving useful language regularly, not to memorise every unfamiliar word.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: A second conditional imagines a situation: If I had more time, I would practise daily. Unlike a first conditional, it does not present the condition as a straightforward expectation. Advice should respond to the actual difficulty. Separate a strategy's purpose from a guaranteed result.","description":"Explain problems, recommend strategies and discuss hypothetical choices. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What is Priya's main difficulty?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Reading any article"},{"id":"o1","label":"Recalling words while speaking"},{"id":"o2","label":"Using a notebook"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She understands slow reading but struggles with retrieval in conversation.","passage":"Priya can understand articles when she reads slowly, but she forgets new words during conversation. Her tutor suggests keeping a small notebook with phrases rather than isolated translations. Priya also records a short summary after reading and listens to it the next day. The tutor does not promise instant fluency. The aim is to practise retrieving useful language regularly, not to memorise every unfamiliar word.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What does the tutor recommend?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Learning every unknown word"},{"id":"o1","label":"Avoiding conversation"},{"id":"o2","label":"Recording useful phrases and practising retrieval"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The notebook and summaries support repeated retrieval, not exhaustive memorisation.","passage":"Priya can understand articles when she reads slowly, but she forgets new words during conversation. Her tutor suggests keeping a small notebook with phrases rather than isolated translations. Priya also records a short summary after reading and listens to it the next day. The tutor does not promise instant fluency. The aim is to practise retrieving useful language regularly, not to memorise every unfamiliar word.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A second conditional imagines a situation: If I had more time, I would practise daily. Unlike a first conditional, it does not present the condition as a straightforward expectation. Advice should respond to the actual difficulty. Separate a strategy's purpose from a guaranteed result.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: If I had more time, I ___ practise every day.","solution":"would","explanation":"A hypothetical second conditional uses would in its result clause.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: If she ___ (be) more confident, she would speak more. Use the formal hypothetical form.","solution":"were","explanation":"Were is the formal hypothetical form with all subjects.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"A second conditional imagines a situation: If I had more time, I would practise daily. Unlike a first conditional, it does not present the condition as a straightforward expectation. Advice should respond to the actual difficulty. Separate a strategy's purpose from a guaranteed result.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose targeted advice.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"You should never study vocabulary."},{"id":"o1","label":"Why not practise a few useful phrases in short conversations?"},{"id":"o2","label":"Buy a larger dictionary and stop speaking."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The suggestion addresses active retrieval with a realistic task.","passage":"Classmate: I study long lists, but I cannot use the words when speaking. You want to suggest a manageable alternative.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A second conditional imagines a situation: If I had more time, I would practise daily. Unlike a first conditional, it does not present the condition as a straightforward expectation. Advice should respond to the actual difficulty. Separate a strategy's purpose from a guaranteed result.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What changed in the study method?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"More highlighting"},{"id":"o1","label":"Explaining without looking at the book"},{"id":"o2","label":"Avoiding difficult sections"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The new method involves retrieval and explanation.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-learning"},{"prompt":"What benefit does the speaker report?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Finding gaps sooner"},{"id":"o1","label":"Never forgetting anything"},{"id":"o2","label":"Finishing every book faster"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reported benefit is detecting gaps, not guaranteed perfect memory.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-learning"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What is Priya's main difficulty?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Reading any article"},{"id":"o1","label":"Recalling words while speaking"},{"id":"o2","label":"Using a notebook"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"She understands slow reading but struggles with retrieval in conversation.","passage":"Priya can understand articles when she reads slowly, but she forgets new words during conversation. Her tutor suggests keeping a small notebook with phrases rather than isolated translations. Priya also records a short summary after reading and listens to it the next day. The tutor does not promise instant fluency. The aim is to practise retrieving useful language regularly, not to memorise every unfamiliar word.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What does the tutor recommend?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Learning every unknown word"},{"id":"o1","label":"Avoiding conversation"},{"id":"o2","label":"Recording useful phrases and practising retrieval"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The notebook and summaries support repeated retrieval, not exhaustive memorisation.","passage":"Priya can understand articles when she reads slowly, but she forgets new words during conversation. Her tutor suggests keeping a small notebook with phrases rather than isolated translations. Priya also records a short summary after reading and listens to it the next day. The tutor does not promise instant fluency. The aim is to practise retrieving useful language regularly, not to memorise every unfamiliar word.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose targeted advice.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"You should never study vocabulary."},{"id":"o1","label":"Why not practise a few useful phrases in short conversations?"},{"id":"o2","label":"Buy a larger dictionary and stop speaking."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The suggestion addresses active retrieval with a realistic task.","passage":"Classmate: I study long lists, but I cannot use the words when speaking. You want to suggest a manageable alternative.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"A second conditional imagines a situation: If I had more time, I would practise daily. Unlike a first conditional, it does not present the condition as a straightforward expectation. Advice should respond to the actual difficulty. Separate a strategy's purpose from a guaranteed result.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b1-work","level":"B1","title":"Work and practical communication","objective":"Report requests, clarify responsibilities and use common passive forms.","project_prompt":"Write 120–180 words explaining a comparable problem, its background and a proposed response. Connect events and reasons; consider an alternative.\nTopic: Work and practical communication\nFocus: Report requests, clarify responsibilities and use common passive forms.\nSource context: At a small design studio, customer requests are entered into a shared system. Each morning, Lena checks new requests and assigns urgent ones to a designer. Yesterday, a client asked the team to change a delivery address. Lena updated the record but did not tell the courier. The team now sends a separate confirmation whenever an address changes, because updating the system alone did not reach everyone involved.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: The passive focuses on an action or recipient: the form is checked every morning. Use be plus past participle. Reported requests can use asked someone to do something. In practical messages, state the task, deadline and responsible person; ask for clarification rather than assume.","description":"Report requests, clarify responsibilities and use common passive forms. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why was the process changed?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The shared system had no records."},{"id":"o1","label":"A record update did not inform the courier."},{"id":"o2","label":"Designers refused urgent work."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The communication gap was between the system update and the courier.","passage":"At a small design studio, customer requests are entered into a shared system. Each morning, Lena checks new requests and assigns urgent ones to a designer. Yesterday, a client asked the team to change a delivery address. Lena updated the record but did not tell the courier. The team now sends a separate confirmation whenever an address changes, because updating the system alone did not reach everyone involved.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is the new step?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A separate confirmation for address changes"},{"id":"o1","label":"Deleting old customer records"},{"id":"o2","label":"Asking every customer to visit"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The team adds confirmation rather than abandoning the system.","passage":"At a small design studio, customer requests are entered into a shared system. Each morning, Lena checks new requests and assigns urgent ones to a designer. Yesterday, a client asked the team to change a delivery address. Lena updated the record but did not tell the courier. The team now sends a separate confirmation whenever an address changes, because updating the system alone did not reach everyone involved.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"The passive focuses on an action or recipient: the form is checked every morning. Use be plus past participle. Reported requests can use asked someone to do something. In practical messages, state the task, deadline and responsible person; ask for clarification rather than assume.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Change to passive: The team checks the form. Begin: The form...","solution":"The form is checked by the team.","explanation":"Present-simple passive uses is checked; retain the agent as requested.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete the reported request: The client asked us ___ change the address.","solution":"to","explanation":"Ask someone to do something reports a request.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"The passive focuses on an action or recipient: the form is checked every morning. Use be plus past participle. Reported requests can use asked someone to do something. In practical messages, state the task, deadline and responsible person; ask for clarification rather than assume.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which clarification is most useful?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Soon means whenever I want."},{"id":"o1","label":"Should I prioritise this over the report, and when do you need it?"},{"id":"o2","label":"You never give good instructions."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The question clarifies both priority and timing without blame.","passage":"Manager: Please finish this soon. You have two urgent tasks and need a precise priority and deadline.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"The passive focuses on an action or recipient: the form is checked every morning. Use be plus past participle. Reported requests can use asked someone to do something. In practical messages, state the task, deadline and responsible person; ask for clarification rather than assume.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Who receives the draft first?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The client"},{"id":"o1","label":"The speaker"},{"id":"o2","label":"The courier"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The draft goes to the speaker for checking.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-work"},{"prompt":"When can the approved version be sent?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Wednesday"},{"id":"o1","label":"Thursday morning"},{"id":"o2","label":"Friday"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"Friday follows the check of the draft.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-work"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why was the process changed?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The shared system had no records."},{"id":"o1","label":"A record update did not inform the courier."},{"id":"o2","label":"Designers refused urgent work."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The communication gap was between the system update and the courier.","passage":"At a small design studio, customer requests are entered into a shared system. Each morning, Lena checks new requests and assigns urgent ones to a designer. Yesterday, a client asked the team to change a delivery address. Lena updated the record but did not tell the courier. The team now sends a separate confirmation whenever an address changes, because updating the system alone did not reach everyone involved.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is the new step?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A separate confirmation for address changes"},{"id":"o1","label":"Deleting old customer records"},{"id":"o2","label":"Asking every customer to visit"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The team adds confirmation rather than abandoning the system.","passage":"At a small design studio, customer requests are entered into a shared system. Each morning, Lena checks new requests and assigns urgent ones to a designer. Yesterday, a client asked the team to change a delivery address. Lena updated the record but did not tell the courier. The team now sends a separate confirmation whenever an address changes, because updating the system alone did not reach everyone involved.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which clarification is most useful?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Soon means whenever I want."},{"id":"o1","label":"Should I prioritise this over the report, and when do you need it?"},{"id":"o2","label":"You never give good instructions."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The question clarifies both priority and timing without blame.","passage":"Manager: Please finish this soon. You have two urgent tasks and need a precise priority and deadline.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"The passive focuses on an action or recipient: the form is checked every morning. Use be plus past participle. Reported requests can use asked someone to do something. In practical messages, state the task, deadline and responsible person; ask for clarification rather than assume.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b1-community","level":"B1","title":"Community and consequences","objective":"Explain social proposals using reasons, results and relative clauses.","project_prompt":"Write 120–180 words explaining a comparable problem, its background and a proposed response. Connect events and reasons; consider an alternative.\nTopic: Community and consequences\nFocus: Explain social proposals using reasons, results and relative clauses.\nSource context: Residents have proposed turning an unused car park into a community garden. People who live in nearby flats would gain a place to grow vegetables. However, a local shop uses the car park for deliveries twice a week. The council has not made a decision. It has invited residents and shop owners to discuss a design that keeps a small delivery area while creating space for planting.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Use who for people and which for things in defining relative clauses. Because introduces a reason; so introduces a result. A proposal can have benefits and costs at the same time. When summarising a local issue, identify who is affected and distinguish the proposal from an agreed decision.","description":"Explain social proposals using reasons, results and relative clauses. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the council's current position?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It has rejected all gardens."},{"id":"o1","label":"It has already built the garden."},{"id":"o2","label":"It is consulting before deciding."}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The council has invited discussion and has not decided.","passage":"Residents have proposed turning an unused car park into a community garden. People who live in nearby flats would gain a place to grow vegetables. However, a local shop uses the car park for deliveries twice a week. The council has not made a decision. It has invited residents and shop owners to discuss a design that keeps a small delivery area while creating space for planting.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which compromise is proposed?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Keeping a delivery area alongside planting space"},{"id":"o1","label":"Stopping all deliveries"},{"id":"o2","label":"Removing all nearby flats"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The design attempts to meet both groups' needs.","passage":"Residents have proposed turning an unused car park into a community garden. People who live in nearby flats would gain a place to grow vegetables. However, a local shop uses the car park for deliveries twice a week. The council has not made a decision. It has invited residents and shop owners to discuss a design that keeps a small delivery area while creating space for planting.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use who for people and which for things in defining relative clauses. Because introduces a reason; so introduces a result. A proposal can have benefits and costs at the same time. When summarising a local issue, identify who is affected and distinguish the proposal from an agreed decision.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: Residents ___ live nearby can join. Use who or which.","solution":"who","explanation":"Who introduces a defining clause about people.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Join using because: The shop needs access. It receives deliveries. Begin: The shop needs access...","solution":"The shop needs access because it receives deliveries.","explanation":"Because introduces the reason for needing access.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use who for people and which for things in defining relative clauses. Because introduces a reason; so introduces a result. A proposal can have benefits and costs at the same time. When summarising a local issue, identify who is affected and distinguish the proposal from an agreed decision.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose a constructive response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"There are no practical problems."},{"id":"o1","label":"We can support the garden and still discuss access for deliveries."},{"id":"o2","label":"All shops should close immediately."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply rejects a false either-or choice and focuses on a workable concern.","passage":"At a meeting, someone says: Anyone who questions the garden is against the community. You support the garden but recognise delivery needs.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Use who for people and which for things in defining relative clauses. Because introduces a reason; so introduces a result. A proposal can have benefits and costs at the same time. When summarising a local issue, identify who is affected and distinguish the proposal from an agreed decision.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Who will manage borrowing?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Volunteers alone"},{"id":"o1","label":"Paid staff"},{"id":"o2","label":"Visitors"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Paid staff retain responsibility for borrowing.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-community"},{"prompt":"Is the change permanent?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Yes, without review"},{"id":"o1","label":"No, it ends tomorrow"},{"id":"o2","label":"Not yet; attendance will be reviewed"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The three-month trial precedes a decision.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-community"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the council's current position?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It has rejected all gardens."},{"id":"o1","label":"It has already built the garden."},{"id":"o2","label":"It is consulting before deciding."}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The council has invited discussion and has not decided.","passage":"Residents have proposed turning an unused car park into a community garden. People who live in nearby flats would gain a place to grow vegetables. However, a local shop uses the car park for deliveries twice a week. The council has not made a decision. It has invited residents and shop owners to discuss a design that keeps a small delivery area while creating space for planting.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which compromise is proposed?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Keeping a delivery area alongside planting space"},{"id":"o1","label":"Stopping all deliveries"},{"id":"o2","label":"Removing all nearby flats"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The design attempts to meet both groups' needs.","passage":"Residents have proposed turning an unused car park into a community garden. People who live in nearby flats would gain a place to grow vegetables. However, a local shop uses the car park for deliveries twice a week. The council has not made a decision. It has invited residents and shop owners to discuss a design that keeps a small delivery area while creating space for planting.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose a constructive response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"There are no practical problems."},{"id":"o1","label":"We can support the garden and still discuss access for deliveries."},{"id":"o2","label":"All shops should close immediately."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply rejects a false either-or choice and focuses on a workable concern.","passage":"At a meeting, someone says: Anyone who questions the garden is against the community. You support the garden but recognise delivery needs.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Use who for people and which for things in defining relative clauses. Because introduces a reason; so introduces a result. A proposal can have benefits and costs at the same time. When summarising a local issue, identify who is affected and distinguish the proposal from an agreed decision.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b1-media","level":"B1","title":"News, claims and sources","objective":"Distinguish a reported claim from verified information.","project_prompt":"Write 120–180 words explaining a comparable problem, its background and a proposed response. Connect events and reasons; consider an alternative.\nTopic: News, claims and sources\nFocus: Distinguish a reported claim from verified information.\nSource context: A headline claimed that a town had stopped using plastic bags. The article explained that three supermarkets had removed free bags, but smaller shops could still sell them. A campaign group said the change would reduce waste. The council had not yet measured the effect. Mia shared the article with a note explaining the limited scope, rather than repeating the headline as if it described every shop.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Reported speech often shifts present to past when reporting from a later viewpoint. Said is followed by a clause; told normally needs an object. A headline may state a claim more strongly than the article supports. Check who supplied a figure, when it was measured and whether another source confirms it.","description":"Distinguish a reported claim from verified information. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What did the headline exaggerate?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The scope of the change"},{"id":"o1","label":"The number of residents"},{"id":"o2","label":"The price of bread"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Only three supermarkets changed their practice; the headline implied the whole town.","passage":"A headline claimed that a town had stopped using plastic bags. The article explained that three supermarkets had removed free bags, but smaller shops could still sell them. A campaign group said the change would reduce waste. The council had not yet measured the effect. Mia shared the article with a note explaining the limited scope, rather than repeating the headline as if it described every shop.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What remains unknown?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether the campaign exists"},{"id":"o1","label":"The measured effect on waste"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether shops sell food"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The council had not yet measured the effect.","passage":"A headline claimed that a town had stopped using plastic bags. The article explained that three supermarkets had removed free bags, but smaller shops could still sell them. A campaign group said the change would reduce waste. The council had not yet measured the effect. Mia shared the article with a note explaining the limited scope, rather than repeating the headline as if it described every shop.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Reported speech often shifts present to past when reporting from a later viewpoint. Said is followed by a clause; told normally needs an object. A headline may state a claim more strongly than the article supports. Check who supplied a figure, when it was measured and whether another source confirms it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Report yesterday's words using backshift: She said, 'I am tired.' Begin: She said that...","solution":"She said that she was tired.","explanation":"Am becomes was in the requested backshift.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete with said or told: He ___ me that the shop was closed.","solution":"told","explanation":"Told takes the object me.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Reported speech often shifts present to past when reporting from a later viewpoint. Said is followed by a clause; told normally needs an object. A headline may state a claim more strongly than the article supports. Check who supplied a figure, when it was measured and whether another source confirms it.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the accurate correction.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The article only describes three supermarkets, so the headline is broader than the evidence."},{"id":"o1","label":"Every headline is always false."},{"id":"o2","label":"There is no way to check any claim."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response identifies the specific mismatch without dismissing all reporting.","passage":"Friend: This headline proves every shop has banned bags. You have read the full article and want to correct the claim politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Reported speech often shifts present to past when reporting from a later viewpoint. Said is followed by a clause; told normally needs an object. A headline may state a claim more strongly than the article supports. Check who supplied a figure, when it was measured and whether another source confirms it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What limits the survey?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It asked nobody"},{"id":"o1","label":"A small, specific group responded"},{"id":"o2","label":"Every resident responded"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Eighty respondents, mostly commuters, may not represent all residents.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-media"},{"prompt":"What does sixty percent refer to?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"All residents"},{"id":"o1","label":"All tourists"},{"id":"o2","label":"People who responded"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The percentage describes respondents, not the entire population.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-media"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What did the headline exaggerate?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The scope of the change"},{"id":"o1","label":"The number of residents"},{"id":"o2","label":"The price of bread"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Only three supermarkets changed their practice; the headline implied the whole town.","passage":"A headline claimed that a town had stopped using plastic bags. The article explained that three supermarkets had removed free bags, but smaller shops could still sell them. A campaign group said the change would reduce waste. The council had not yet measured the effect. Mia shared the article with a note explaining the limited scope, rather than repeating the headline as if it described every shop.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What remains unknown?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether the campaign exists"},{"id":"o1","label":"The measured effect on waste"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether shops sell food"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The council had not yet measured the effect.","passage":"A headline claimed that a town had stopped using plastic bags. The article explained that three supermarkets had removed free bags, but smaller shops could still sell them. A campaign group said the change would reduce waste. The council had not yet measured the effect. Mia shared the article with a note explaining the limited scope, rather than repeating the headline as if it described every shop.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the accurate correction.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The article only describes three supermarkets, so the headline is broader than the evidence."},{"id":"o1","label":"Every headline is always false."},{"id":"o2","label":"There is no way to check any claim."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response identifies the specific mismatch without dismissing all reporting.","passage":"Friend: This headline proves every shop has banned bags. You have read the full article and want to correct the claim politely.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Reported speech often shifts present to past when reporting from a later viewpoint. Said is followed by a clause; told normally needs an object. A headline may state a claim more strongly than the article supports. Check who supplied a figure, when it was measured and whether another source confirms it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b1-decisions","level":"B1","title":"Decisions and possible outcomes","objective":"Discuss alternatives, hypothetical outcomes and practical trade-offs.","project_prompt":"Write 120–180 words explaining a comparable problem, its background and a proposed response. Connect events and reasons; consider an alternative.\nTopic: Decisions and possible outcomes\nFocus: Discuss alternatives, hypothetical outcomes and practical trade-offs.\nSource context: Rafael has been offered a job in another city. The salary is higher, but rent would also cost more. His current job is close to his family and allows flexible hours. He asks the new employer whether he could work remotely two days a week. If the employer agrees, he will accept. If he had no family responsibilities, he would be more willing to move immediately.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: First conditionals discuss plausible future conditions; second conditionals imagine less direct or unreal situations. Might expresses possibility, not certainty. A trade-off means gaining one benefit while accepting a cost. Recommend an option in relation to stated needs rather than assuming one choice is best for everyone.","description":"Discuss alternatives, hypothetical outcomes and practical trade-offs. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What condition determines his decision?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether remote work is allowed"},{"id":"o1","label":"Whether the city has shops"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether his current salary falls"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"He will accept if the employer agrees to two remote days.","passage":"Rafael has been offered a job in another city. The salary is higher, but rent would also cost more. His current job is close to his family and allows flexible hours. He asks the new employer whether he could work remotely two days a week. If the employer agrees, he will accept. If he had no family responsibilities, he would be more willing to move immediately.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Why is the higher salary not enough by itself?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He does not need money."},{"id":"o1","label":"Rent and family responsibilities also matter."},{"id":"o2","label":"The offer has been withdrawn."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The decision includes financial costs and family needs.","passage":"Rafael has been offered a job in another city. The salary is higher, but rent would also cost more. His current job is close to his family and allows flexible hours. He asks the new employer whether he could work remotely two days a week. If the employer agrees, he will accept. If he had no family responsibilities, he would be more willing to move immediately.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"First conditionals discuss plausible future conditions; second conditionals imagine less direct or unreal situations. Might expresses possibility, not certainty. A trade-off means gaining one benefit while accepting a cost. Recommend an option in relation to stated needs rather than assuming one choice is best for everyone.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: If they agree, I ___ accept. Use will or would.","solution":"will","explanation":"The first conditional presents a real future possibility.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: If I had fewer responsibilities, I ___ move. Use will or would.","solution":"would","explanation":"The second conditional imagines a different present situation.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"First conditionals discuss plausible future conditions; second conditionals imagine less direct or unreal situations. Might expresses possibility, not certainty. A trade-off means gaining one benefit while accepting a cost. Recommend an option in relation to stated needs rather than assuming one choice is best for everyone.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose a professional reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Salary is the only thing that matters."},{"id":"o1","label":"I appreciate the offer. Could we clarify the remote-working arrangement before I decide?"},{"id":"o2","label":"There is nothing to discuss."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The response acknowledges the offer while requesting necessary information.","passage":"Recruiter: The salary is higher, so surely you can accept today. You want to discuss an important condition first.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"First conditionals discuss plausible future conditions; second conditionals imagine less direct or unreal situations. Might expresses possibility, not certainty. A trade-off means gaining one benefit while accepting a cost. Recommend an option in relation to stated needs rather than assuming one choice is best for everyone.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What benefit influenced the choice?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Keeping the job"},{"id":"o1","label":"Avoiding all travel"},{"id":"o2","label":"Finishing in one week"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The evening course allows continued employment.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-decisions"},{"prompt":"What is the disadvantage?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"No teaching"},{"id":"o1","label":"Late journeys home"},{"id":"o2","label":"A cancelled weekend course"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker finds the late journeys tiring.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b1-decisions"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What condition determines his decision?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether remote work is allowed"},{"id":"o1","label":"Whether the city has shops"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether his current salary falls"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"He will accept if the employer agrees to two remote days.","passage":"Rafael has been offered a job in another city. The salary is higher, but rent would also cost more. His current job is close to his family and allows flexible hours. He asks the new employer whether he could work remotely two days a week. If the employer agrees, he will accept. If he had no family responsibilities, he would be more willing to move immediately.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Why is the higher salary not enough by itself?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"He does not need money."},{"id":"o1","label":"Rent and family responsibilities also matter."},{"id":"o2","label":"The offer has been withdrawn."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The decision includes financial costs and family needs.","passage":"Rafael has been offered a job in another city. The salary is higher, but rent would also cost more. His current job is close to his family and allows flexible hours. He asks the new employer whether he could work remotely two days a week. If the employer agrees, he will accept. If he had no family responsibilities, he would be more willing to move immediately.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose a professional reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Salary is the only thing that matters."},{"id":"o1","label":"I appreciate the offer. Could we clarify the remote-working arrangement before I decide?"},{"id":"o2","label":"There is nothing to discuss."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The response acknowledges the offer while requesting necessary information.","passage":"Recruiter: The salary is higher, so surely you can accept today. You want to discuss an important condition first.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"First conditionals discuss plausible future conditions; second conditionals imagine less direct or unreal situations. Might expresses possibility, not certainty. A trade-off means gaining one benefit while accepting a cost. Recommend an option in relation to stated needs rather than assuming one choice is best for everyone.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b2-argument","level":"B2","title":"Developing a balanced argument","objective":"Evaluate competing arguments, concessions and qualified conclusions.","project_prompt":"Write 180–250 words presenting a reasoned response to the unit issue. Develop a position, engage with a counterargument, and qualify the conclusion.\nTopic: Developing a balanced argument\nFocus: Evaluate competing arguments, concessions and qualified conclusions.\nSource context: A university is considering recording all lectures. Supporters argue that recordings help students who are ill or need to revisit difficult material. Critics worry that attendance could decline and discussion could become less spontaneous. A pilot found no overall fall in attendance, although participation varied by subject. The report recommends offering recordings alongside in-person teaching rather than treating them as a replacement. It also notes that a one-term pilot cannot establish every long-term effect.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: A balanced argument concedes a reasonable opposing point before explaining its limits. Although introduces a concession, whereas contrasts alternatives. Avoid changing may into will when summarising a claim. A conclusion should reflect the strength and scope of the evidence, not just repeat the most forceful opinion.","description":"Evaluate competing arguments, concessions and qualified conclusions. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which conclusion matches the report?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Replace all teaching with recordings."},{"id":"o1","label":"Use recordings as a supplement while monitoring effects."},{"id":"o2","label":"Recordings always reduce attendance."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The report supports supplementation and qualifies the pilot's scope.","passage":"A university is considering recording all lectures. Supporters argue that recordings help students who are ill or need to revisit difficult material. Critics worry that attendance could decline and discussion could become less spontaneous. A pilot found no overall fall in attendance, although participation varied by subject. The report recommends offering recordings alongside in-person teaching rather than treating them as a replacement. It also notes that a one-term pilot cannot establish every long-term effect.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What does the pilot fail to establish?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"All long-term effects"},{"id":"o1","label":"Whether recordings existed"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether students can be ill"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"A single term does not establish all long-term outcomes.","passage":"A university is considering recording all lectures. Supporters argue that recordings help students who are ill or need to revisit difficult material. Critics worry that attendance could decline and discussion could become less spontaneous. A pilot found no overall fall in attendance, although participation varied by subject. The report recommends offering recordings alongside in-person teaching rather than treating them as a replacement. It also notes that a one-term pilot cannot establish every long-term effect.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A balanced argument concedes a reasonable opposing point before explaining its limits. Although introduces a concession, whereas contrasts alternatives. Avoid changing may into will when summarising a claim. A conclusion should reflect the strength and scope of the evidence, not just repeat the most forceful opinion.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Combine using although: Attendance remained stable. Participation varied. Begin: Although participation...","solution":"Although participation varied, attendance remained stable.","explanation":"Although concedes variation while preserving the main finding.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: Some students prefer recordings, ___ others value live discussion. Use whereas or because.","solution":"whereas","explanation":"Whereas contrasts preferences rather than stating a cause.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"A balanced argument concedes a reasonable opposing point before explaining its limits. Although introduces a concession, whereas contrasts alternatives. Avoid changing may into will when summarising a claim. A conclusion should reflect the strength and scope of the evidence, not just repeat the most forceful opinion.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the best qualification.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The pilot found no overall decline, but that does not settle every long-term question."},{"id":"o1","label":"The pilot contains no information at all."},{"id":"o2","label":"Attendance must always fall."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply retains the finding while limiting the inference.","passage":"Colleague: The pilot proves recordings can never harm attendance. You want to preserve the finding without overstating it.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A balanced argument concedes a reasonable opposing point before explaining its limits. Although introduces a concession, whereas contrasts alternatives. Avoid changing may into will when summarising a claim. A conclusion should reflect the strength and scope of the evidence, not just repeat the most forceful opinion.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the speaker's stance?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Total opposition"},{"id":"o1","label":"Qualified support"},{"id":"o2","label":"Unconditional acceptance"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Support in principle is paired with a concern about uniform implementation.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-argument"},{"prompt":"What is proposed?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Immediate identical implementation"},{"id":"o1","label":"Abandoning the goal"},{"id":"o2","label":"Testing different departmental approaches"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The speaker favours local trials before a uniform policy.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-argument"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation 