question."},{"id":"o1","label":"The pilot contains no information at all."},{"id":"o2","label":"Attendance must always fall."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply retains the finding while limiting the inference.","passage":"Colleague: The pilot proves recordings can never harm attendance. You want to preserve the finding without overstating it.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"A balanced argument concedes a reasonable opposing point before explaining its limits. Although introduces a concession, whereas contrasts alternatives. Avoid changing may into will when summarising a claim. A conclusion should reflect the strength and scope of the evidence, not just repeat the most forceful opinion.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b2-evidence","level":"B2","title":"Evidence, likelihood and causation","objective":"Interpret uncertainty and distinguish association from causation.","project_prompt":"Write 180–250 words presenting a reasoned response to the unit issue. Develop a position, engage with a counterargument, and qualify the conclusion.\nTopic: Evidence, likelihood and causation\nFocus: Interpret uncertainty and distinguish association from causation.\nSource context: An article reported that employees who cycled to work took fewer sick days. The study followed volunteers rather than randomly assigning commuting methods. Cyclists may have differed in health or working patterns before the study began. The researchers described an association and called for further investigation. A company newsletter nevertheless announced that buying a bicycle would prevent illness. That claim turns a group-level observation into an individual guarantee the research did not provide.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: May, might and could signal possibility; must can express a strong deduction, not only obligation. An association does not by itself establish that one factor caused another. Compare groups, alternative explanations and limits of measurement. Use appears to and suggests when stronger certainty would exceed the evidence.","description":"Interpret uncertainty and distinguish association from causation. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why is the newsletter's conclusion too strong?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It promises causation and certainty not established by the study."},{"id":"o1","label":"The study did not involve employees."},{"id":"o2","label":"Nobody in the study cycled."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"An observational association cannot justify an individual guarantee.","passage":"An article reported that employees who cycled to work took fewer sick days. The study followed volunteers rather than randomly assigning commuting methods. Cyclists may have differed in health or working patterns before the study began. The researchers described an association and called for further investigation. A company newsletter nevertheless announced that buying a bicycle would prevent illness. That claim turns a group-level observation into an individual guarantee the research did not provide.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which alternative explanation is mentioned?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Cyclists may already have differed in health."},{"id":"o1","label":"Sick leave had been banned."},{"id":"o2","label":"The bicycles were randomised."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Pre-existing differences could contribute to the association.","passage":"An article reported that employees who cycled to work took fewer sick days. The study followed volunteers rather than randomly assigning commuting methods. Cyclists may have differed in health or working patterns before the study began. The researchers described an association and called for further investigation. A company newsletter nevertheless announced that buying a bicycle would prevent illness. That claim turns a group-level observation into an individual guarantee the research did not provide.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"May, might and could signal possibility; must can express a strong deduction, not only obligation. An association does not by itself establish that one factor caused another. Compare groups, alternative explanations and limits of measurement. Use appears to and suggests when stronger certainty would exceed the evidence.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with may or must: The evidence is limited; the difference ___ reflect prior health.","solution":"may","explanation":"May expresses a possible explanation without certainty.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Use a perfect modal deduction: The office is dark and empty. They ___ (leave). Use must.","solution":"must have left","explanation":"Must have plus past participle expresses a strong deduction about the past.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"May, might and could signal possibility; must can express a strong deduction, not only obligation. An association does not by itself establish that one factor caused another. Compare groups, alternative explanations and limits of measurement. Use appears to and suggests when stronger certainty would exceed the evidence.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose a responsible recommendation.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Say the study found an association, and explain its limits."},{"id":"o1","label":"Guarantee that no cyclist becomes ill."},{"id":"o2","label":"Remove all mention of the study's design."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The recommendation preserves the finding without inventing causal certainty.","passage":"Editor: Can we write that cycling prevents illness? You have only the observational study described above.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"May, might and could signal possibility; must can express a strong deduction, not only obligation. An association does not by itself establish that one factor caused another. Compare groups, alternative explanations and limits of measurement. Use appears to and suggests when stronger certainty would exceed the evidence.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What complicates the interpretation?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"No sales were measured."},{"id":"o1","label":"A discount campaign occurred at the same time."},{"id":"o2","label":"The website was not redesigned."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The concurrent campaign is a competing explanation.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-evidence"},{"prompt":"What additional evidence is requested?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A period without discounts"},{"id":"o1","label":"A stronger slogan"},{"id":"o2","label":"An unqualified prediction"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The comparison could help separate the redesign from the discount effect.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-evidence"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why is the newsletter's conclusion too strong?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It promises causation and certainty not established by the study."},{"id":"o1","label":"The study did not involve employees."},{"id":"o2","label":"Nobody in the study cycled."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"An observational association cannot justify an individual guarantee.","passage":"An article reported that employees who cycled to work took fewer sick days. The study followed volunteers rather than randomly assigning commuting methods. Cyclists may have differed in health or working patterns before the study began. The researchers described an association and called for further investigation. A company newsletter nevertheless announced that buying a bicycle would prevent illness. That claim turns a group-level observation into an individual guarantee the research did not provide.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which alternative explanation is mentioned?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Cyclists may already have differed in health."},{"id":"o1","label":"Sick leave had been banned."},{"id":"o2","label":"The bicycles were randomised."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Pre-existing differences could contribute to the association.","passage":"An article reported that employees who cycled to work took fewer sick days. The study followed volunteers rather than randomly assigning commuting methods. Cyclists may have differed in health or working patterns before the study began. The researchers described an association and called for further investigation. A company newsletter nevertheless announced that buying a bicycle would prevent illness. That claim turns a group-level observation into an individual guarantee the research did not provide.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose a responsible recommendation.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Say the study found an association, and explain its limits."},{"id":"o1","label":"Guarantee that no cyclist becomes ill."},{"id":"o2","label":"Remove all mention of the study's design."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The recommendation preserves the finding without inventing causal certainty.","passage":"Editor: Can we write that cycling prevents illness? You have only the observational study described above.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"May, might and could signal possibility; must can express a strong deduction, not only obligation. An association does not by itself establish that one factor caused another. Compare groups, alternative explanations and limits of measurement. Use appears to and suggests when stronger certainty would exceed the evidence.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b2-negotiation","level":"B2","title":"Negotiating workable agreements","objective":"Express conditions, concessions and boundaries in a negotiation.","project_prompt":"Write 180–250 words presenting a reasoned response to the unit issue. Develop a position, engage with a counterargument, and qualify the conclusion.\nTopic: Negotiating workable agreements\nFocus: Express conditions, concessions and boundaries in a negotiation.\nSource context: A supplier can deliver equipment two weeks earlier if the buyer accepts a smaller first shipment. The buyer needs at least forty units for a launch but can wait for the remaining sixty. The supplier initially offers thirty units. After checking stock, it agrees to forty, provided that the buyer confirms by noon. The buyer asks for the revised dates and quantities in writing before signing. Neither side gets its original ideal, but both protect their main operational need.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Provided that and as long as introduce conditions. Would be willing to expresses a conditional offer, not an existing agreement. A concession gives something up to obtain something else. Distinguish a non-negotiable constraint from a preference, and confirm the final terms rather than assuming silence means acceptance.","description":"Express conditions, concessions and boundaries in a negotiation. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the buyer's essential requirement?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"All hundred units immediately"},{"id":"o1","label":"At least forty units for launch"},{"id":"o2","label":"Only thirty units ever"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Forty units are needed for launch; sixty may arrive later.","passage":"A supplier can deliver equipment two weeks earlier if the buyer accepts a smaller first shipment. The buyer needs at least forty units for a launch but can wait for the remaining sixty. The supplier initially offers thirty units. After checking stock, it agrees to forty, provided that the buyer confirms by noon. The buyer asks for the revised dates and quantities in writing before signing. Neither side gets its original ideal, but both protect their main operational need.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What remains necessary before signing?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Written confirmation of revised terms"},{"id":"o1","label":"A completely different supplier"},{"id":"o2","label":"Silence until next week"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The buyer requests written dates and quantities.","passage":"A supplier can deliver equipment two weeks earlier if the buyer accepts a smaller first shipment. The buyer needs at least forty units for a launch but can wait for the remaining sixty. The supplier initially offers thirty units. After checking stock, it agrees to forty, provided that the buyer confirms by noon. The buyer asks for the revised dates and quantities in writing before signing. Neither side gets its original ideal, but both protect their main operational need.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Provided that and as long as introduce conditions. Would be willing to expresses a conditional offer, not an existing agreement. A concession gives something up to obtain something else. Distinguish a non-negotiable constraint from a preference, and confirm the final terms rather than assuming silence means acceptance.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with provided that or despite: We can deliver early ___ you confirm today.","solution":"provided that","explanation":"Provided that introduces the condition for early delivery.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Correct: We would willing to accept a partial shipment.","solution":"We would be willing to accept a partial shipment.","explanation":"The conditional expression needs be before willing.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Provided that and as long as introduce conditions. Would be willing to expresses a conditional offer, not an existing agreement. A concession gives something up to obtain something else. Distinguish a non-negotiable constraint from a preference, and confirm the final terms rather than assuming silence means acceptance.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the constructive counteroffer.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Thirty is fine even though the launch needs forty."},{"id":"o1","label":"If you can provide forty now, we can accept the remaining sixty later."},{"id":"o2","label":"There is no possible solution."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The offer preserves the minimum while conceding on the rest of the delivery.","passage":"Supplier: We can offer thirty now. Buyer: We need forty for launch. You want to move the negotiation forward without abandoning that minimum.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Provided that and as long as introduce conditions. Would be willing to expresses a conditional offer, not an existing agreement. A concession gives something up to obtain something else. Distinguish a non-negotiable constraint from a preference, and confirm the final terms rather than assuming silence means acceptance.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What condition is attached to a year-long extension?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A lower price"},{"id":"o1","label":"Four-hour support response"},{"id":"o2","label":"No support at all"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The service response time is the stated condition.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-negotiation"},{"prompt":"What is the fallback?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A shorter extension"},{"id":"o1","label":"Automatic cancellation today"},{"id":"o2","label":"A five-year contract"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The speaker prefers a shorter extension if the condition is not met.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-negotiation"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the buyer's essential requirement?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"All hundred units immediately"},{"id":"o1","label":"At least forty units for launch"},{"id":"o2","label":"Only thirty units ever"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Forty units are needed for launch; sixty may arrive later.","passage":"A supplier can deliver equipment two weeks earlier if the buyer accepts a smaller first shipment. The buyer needs at least forty units for a launch but can wait for the remaining sixty. The supplier initially offers thirty units. After checking stock, it agrees to forty, provided that the buyer confirms by noon. The buyer asks for the revised dates and quantities in writing before signing. Neither side gets its original ideal, but both protect their main operational need.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What remains necessary before signing?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Written confirmation of revised terms"},{"id":"o1","label":"A completely different supplier"},{"id":"o2","label":"Silence until next week"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The buyer requests written dates and quantities.","passage":"A supplier can deliver equipment two weeks earlier if the buyer accepts a smaller first shipment. The buyer needs at least forty units for a launch but can wait for the remaining sixty. The supplier initially offers thirty units. After checking stock, it agrees to forty, provided that the buyer confirms by noon. The buyer asks for the revised dates and quantities in writing before signing. Neither side gets its original ideal, but both protect their main operational need.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the constructive counteroffer.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Thirty is fine even though the launch needs forty."},{"id":"o1","label":"If you can provide forty now, we can accept the remaining sixty later."},{"id":"o2","label":"There is no possible solution."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The offer preserves the minimum while conceding on the rest of the delivery.","passage":"Supplier: We can offer thirty now. Buyer: We need forty for launch. You want to move the negotiation forward without abandoning that minimum.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Provided that and as long as introduce conditions. Would be willing to expresses a conditional offer, not an existing agreement. A concession gives something up to obtain something else. Distinguish a non-negotiable constraint from a preference, and confirm the final terms rather than assuming silence means acceptance.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b2-reporting","level":"B2","title":"Reporting events and perspectives","objective":"Use reported speech and passive structures while preserving attribution.","project_prompt":"Write 180–250 words presenting a reasoned response to the unit issue. Develop a position, engage with a counterargument, and qualify the conclusion.\nTopic: Reporting events and perspectives\nFocus: Use reported speech and passive structures while preserving attribution.\nSource context: After a service outage, the operator said that a software update had triggered unexpected errors. An independent review found that testing had not covered a common usage pattern. Customers criticised the delay in communication, rather than the update alone. The final report recommended both broader testing and clearer incident notices. A summary that blamed only individual programmers would omit the review's focus on process and communication.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Report what a source says without silently endorsing it. It is alleged that signals an allegation, not a proven fact. The passive can foreground an event when the agent is unknown or unimportant, but should not conceal responsibility where it matters. Preserve qualifications and differences between sources.","description":"Use reported speech and passive structures while preserving attribution. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What did the independent review identify?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"An untested common usage pattern"},{"id":"o1","label":"Deliberate damage by customers"},{"id":"o2","label":"No software changes"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The review found a gap in test coverage.","passage":"After a service outage, the operator said that a software update had triggered unexpected errors. An independent review found that testing had not covered a common usage pattern. Customers criticised the delay in communication, rather than the update alone. The final report recommended both broader testing and clearer incident notices. A summary that blamed only individual programmers would omit the review's focus on process and communication.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What would a programmer-only explanation miss?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The existence of computers"},{"id":"o1","label":"Process and communication issues"},{"id":"o2","label":"The name of the operator"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The report recommends systemic improvements, not only individual blame.","passage":"After a service outage, the operator said that a software update had triggered unexpected errors. An independent review found that testing had not covered a common usage pattern. Customers criticised the delay in communication, rather than the update alone. The final report recommended both broader testing and clearer incident notices. A summary that blamed only individual programmers would omit the review's focus on process and communication.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Report what a source says without silently endorsing it. It is alleged that signals an allegation, not a proven fact. The passive can foreground an event when the agent is unknown or unimportant, but should not conceal responsibility where it matters. Preserve qualifications and differences between sources.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Report with backshift: The operator said, 'We are investigating.' Begin: The operator said that...","solution":"The operator said that they were investigating.","explanation":"The requested report shifts are investigating to were investigating and we to they.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Change to passive, retaining the agent: The review identified two failures. Begin: Two failures...","solution":"Two failures were identified by the review.","explanation":"Past-simple passive uses were identified.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Report what a source says without silently endorsing it. It is alleged that signals an allegation, not a proven fact. The passive can foreground an event when the agent is unknown or unimportant, but should not conceal responsibility where it matters. Preserve qualifications and differences between sources.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the accurate revision.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Attribute the operator's account and include the review's wider findings."},{"id":"o1","label":"Present the operator's statement as independent proof."},{"id":"o2","label":"Delete the review because it complicates the story."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Attribution and the wider findings prevent a misleading single-cause account.","passage":"A colleague writes: The operator proved the update was the only cause. The source merely says the operator reported a trigger, while a review found wider issues.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Report what a source says without silently endorsing it. It is alleged that signals an allegation, not a proven fact. The passive can foreground an event when the agent is unknown or unimportant, but should not conceal responsibility where it matters. Preserve qualifications and differences between sources.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What happened to the decision?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It was rejected permanently."},{"id":"o1","label":"It was postponed."},{"id":"o2","label":"It passed unanimously."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Postponed is explicitly contrasted with rejected.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-reporting"},{"prompt":"What is still unknown?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The need for cost detail"},{"id":"o1","label":"The chair's existence"},{"id":"o2","label":"The date of the final vote"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"No final voting date has been announced.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-reporting"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What did the independent review identify?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"An untested common usage pattern"},{"id":"o1","label":"Deliberate damage by customers"},{"id":"o2","label":"No software changes"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The review found a gap in test coverage.","passage":"After a service outage, the operator said that a software update had triggered unexpected errors. An independent review found that testing had not covered a common usage pattern. Customers criticised the delay in communication, rather than the update alone. The final report recommended both broader testing and clearer incident notices. A summary that blamed only individual programmers would omit the review's focus on process and communication.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What would a programmer-only explanation miss?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The existence of computers"},{"id":"o1","label":"Process and communication issues"},{"id":"o2","label":"The name of the operator"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The report recommends systemic improvements, not only individual blame.","passage":"After a service outage, the operator said that a software update had triggered unexpected errors. An independent review found that testing had not covered a common usage pattern. Customers criticised the delay in communication, rather than the update alone. The final report recommended both broader testing and clearer incident notices. A summary that blamed only individual programmers would omit the review's focus on process and communication.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the accurate revision.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Attribute the operator's account and include the review's wider findings."},{"id":"o1","label":"Present the operator's statement as independent proof."},{"id":"o2","label":"Delete the review because it complicates the story."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Attribution and the wider findings prevent a misleading single-cause account.","passage":"A colleague writes: The operator proved the update was the only cause. The source merely says the operator reported a trigger, while a review found wider issues.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Report what a source says without silently endorsing it. It is alleged that signals an allegation, not a proven fact. The passive can foreground an event when the agent is unknown or unimportant, but should not conceal responsibility where it matters. Preserve qualifications and differences between sources.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b2-environment","level":"B2","title":"Environment and policy trade-offs","objective":"Evaluate policy choices using conditions, counterfactuals and impacts.","project_prompt":"Write 180–250 words presenting a reasoned response to the unit issue. Develop a position, engage with a counterargument, and qualify the conclusion.\nTopic: Environment and policy trade-offs\nFocus: Evaluate policy choices using conditions, counterfactuals and impacts.\nSource context: A city introduced charges for high-emission vehicles in its centre. Air quality improved during the first year, but small delivery firms reported higher replacement costs. The council offered grants, although applicants criticised a slow process. An evaluation recommended keeping the charge while simplifying grants and improving alternatives for deliveries. It did not conclude that the policy had no costs, nor that every improvement in air quality could be attributed to the charge alone.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Third conditionals consider a different past: If they had invested earlier, costs would have fallen. They do not describe what actually happened. Policy arguments should identify distributional effects: an overall benefit can coexist with costs for a particular group. Distinguish a target, a forecast and an observed outcome.","description":"Evaluate policy choices using conditions, counterfactuals and impacts. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which recommendation reflects the evaluation?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"End the charge immediately."},{"id":"o1","label":"Retain it while improving support and alternatives."},{"id":"o2","label":"Assume every firm benefits equally."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The recommendation combines retention with mitigation of practical burdens.","passage":"A city introduced charges for high-emission vehicles in its centre. Air quality improved during the first year, but small delivery firms reported higher replacement costs. The council offered grants, although applicants criticised a slow process. An evaluation recommended keeping the charge while simplifying grants and improving alternatives for deliveries. It did not conclude that the policy had no costs, nor that every improvement in air quality could be attributed to the charge alone.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which claim is explicitly avoided?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Air quality improved."},{"id":"o1","label":"Some firms faced costs."},{"id":"o2","label":"The charge alone caused all air-quality improvement."}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The text does not attribute every improvement solely to the charge.","passage":"A city introduced charges for high-emission vehicles in its centre. Air quality improved during the first year, but small delivery firms reported higher replacement costs. The council offered grants, although applicants criticised a slow process. An evaluation recommended keeping the charge while simplifying grants and improving alternatives for deliveries. It did not conclude that the policy had no costs, nor that every improvement in air quality could be attributed to the charge alone.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Third conditionals consider a different past: If they had invested earlier, costs would have fallen. They do not describe what actually happened. Policy arguments should identify distributional effects: an overall benefit can coexist with costs for a particular group. Distinguish a target, a forecast and an observed outcome.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the third conditional: If they had invested earlier, costs ___ (fall). Use would.","solution":"would have fallen","explanation":"A past counterfactual result uses would have plus past participle.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: If the grants ___ (arrive) sooner, firms would have replaced vehicles. Use past perfect.","solution":"had arrived","explanation":"Past perfect supplies the unreal past condition.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Third conditionals consider a different past: If they had invested earlier, costs would have fallen. They do not describe what actually happened. Policy arguments should identify distributional effects: an overall benefit can coexist with costs for a particular group. Distinguish a target, a forecast and an observed outcome.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose a balanced reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"An overall benefit does not remove the need to address uneven costs."},{"id":"o1","label":"Any cost proves there was no benefit."},{"id":"o2","label":"No evaluation is necessary."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply distinguishes total benefits from their distribution.","passage":"At a consultation, someone says: Since air improved, we should ignore delivery firms' concerns. You want to acknowledge both effects.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Third conditionals consider a different past: If they had invested earlier, costs would have fallen. They do not describe what actually happened. Policy arguments should identify distributional effects: an overall benefit can coexist with costs for a particular group. Distinguish a target, a forecast and an observed outcome.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What does ten percent represent?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A confirmed annual saving"},{"id":"o1","label":"A target"},{"id":"o2","label":"A rise in demand"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker explicitly labels it as a target.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-environment"},{"prompt":"Why is more data needed?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Winter is not yet represented."},{"id":"o1","label":"Summer readings are all fabricated."},{"id":"o2","label":"The scheme has no purpose."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Seasonal coverage is incomplete.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-environment"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Which recommendation reflects the evaluation?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"End the charge immediately."},{"id":"o1","label":"Retain it while improving support and alternatives."},{"id":"o2","label":"Assume every firm benefits equally."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The recommendation combines retention with mitigation of practical burdens.","passage":"A city introduced charges for high-emission vehicles in its centre. Air quality improved during the first year, but small delivery firms reported higher replacement costs. The council offered grants, although applicants criticised a slow process. An evaluation recommended keeping the charge while simplifying grants and improving alternatives for deliveries. It did not conclude that the policy had no costs, nor that every improvement in air quality could be attributed to the charge alone.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which claim is explicitly avoided?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Air quality improved."},{"id":"o1","label":"Some firms faced costs."},{"id":"o2","label":"The charge alone caused all air-quality improvement."}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The text does not attribute every improvement solely to the charge.","passage":"A city introduced charges for high-emission vehicles in its centre. Air quality improved during the first year, but small delivery firms reported higher replacement costs. The council offered grants, although applicants criticised a slow process. An evaluation recommended keeping the charge while simplifying grants and improving alternatives for deliveries. It did not conclude that the policy had no costs, nor that every improvement in air quality could be attributed to the charge alone.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose a balanced reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"An overall benefit does not remove the need to address uneven costs."},{"id":"o1","label":"Any cost proves there was no benefit."},{"id":"o2","label":"No evaluation is necessary."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply distinguishes total benefits from their distribution.","passage":"At a consultation, someone says: Since air improved, we should ignore delivery firms' concerns. You want to acknowledge both effects.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Third conditionals consider a different past: If they had invested earlier, costs would have fallen. They do not describe what actually happened. Policy arguments should identify distributional effects: an overall benefit can coexist with costs for a particular group. Distinguish a target, a forecast and an observed outcome.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"b2-culture","level":"B2","title":"Culture, interpretation and reviews","objective":"Interpret evaluative language and distinguish taste from reasoned judgement.","project_prompt":"Write 180–250 words presenting a reasoned response to the unit issue. Develop a position, engage with a counterargument, and qualify the conclusion.\nTopic: Culture, interpretation and reviews\nFocus: Interpret evaluative language and distinguish taste from reasoned judgement.\nSource context: The exhibition places everyday objects beside recordings of their owners' memories. One reviewer praises its intimate scale but finds the written labels too vague to explain the historical setting. Another welcomes that openness, arguing that visitors should form their own connections. Both admire the sound design. Their disagreement concerns how much interpretive guidance a museum should provide, rather than whether the objects are valuable or whether the recordings are technically effective.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: A review combines description, evaluation and reasons. An unfamiliar style is not automatically a flaw. Comparisons should identify the criterion used. Not only... but also links parallel elements. A mixed review can praise one aspect while criticising another; do not reduce it to simply positive or negative.","description":"Interpret evaluative language and distinguish taste from reasoned judgement. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the central disagreement?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether the sound works"},{"id":"o1","label":"How much guidance visitors need"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether objects exist"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reviewers differ over interpretive guidance, while agreeing on sound design.","passage":"The exhibition places everyday objects beside recordings of their owners' memories. One reviewer praises its intimate scale but finds the written labels too vague to explain the historical setting. Another welcomes that openness, arguing that visitors should form their own connections. Both admire the sound design. Their disagreement concerns how much interpretive guidance a museum should provide, rather than whether the objects are valuable or whether the recordings are technically effective.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What do both reviewers appreciate?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The sound design"},{"id":"o1","label":"Every written label"},{"id":"o2","label":"The lack of visitors"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The shared positive judgement concerns sound design.","passage":"The exhibition places everyday objects beside recordings of their owners' memories. One reviewer praises its intimate scale but finds the written labels too vague to explain the historical setting. Another welcomes that openness, arguing that visitors should form their own connections. Both admire the sound design. Their disagreement concerns how much interpretive guidance a museum should provide, rather than whether the objects are valuable or whether the recordings are technically effective.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A review combines description, evaluation and reasons. An unfamiliar style is not automatically a flaw. Comparisons should identify the criterion used. Not only... but also links parallel elements. A mixed review can praise one aspect while criticising another; do not reduce it to simply positive or negative.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the parallel structure: The show is not only intimate ___ also thought-provoking.","solution":"but","explanation":"Not only pairs with but also.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Combine with whereas: One reviewer wants more guidance. The other values openness. Begin: One reviewer wants more guidance...","solution":"One reviewer wants more guidance, whereas the other values openness.","explanation":"Whereas makes the contrast explicit.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"A review combines description, evaluation and reasons. An unfamiliar style is not automatically a flaw. Comparisons should identify the criterion used. Not only... but also links parallel elements. A mixed review can praise one aspect while criticising another; do not reduce it to simply positive or negative.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the nuanced response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Could we separate the quality of the work from our preference for more guidance?"},{"id":"o1","label":"Every exhibition is equally good."},{"id":"o2","label":"Visitors should never ask questions."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response distinguishes a criterion or preference from a total verdict.","passage":"Friend: If the labels do not explain everything, the exhibition must be bad. You want to invite a more precise judgement.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A review combines description, evaluation and reasons. An unfamiliar style is not automatically a flaw. Comparisons should identify the criterion used. Not only... but also links parallel elements. A mixed review can praise one aspect while criticising another; do not reduce it to simply positive or negative.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"How does the reviewer assess the slow pace?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"As entirely pointless"},{"id":"o1","label":"As initially difficult but ultimately useful"},{"id":"o2","label":"As evidence of constant action"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The pace initially discourages the reviewer but supports the ending.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-culture"},{"prompt":"Who is the recommended audience?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Readers seeking only action"},{"id":"o1","label":"Readers avoiding characters"},{"id":"o2","label":"Readers who enjoy gradual development"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The recommendation is explicitly qualified by reader preference.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_b2-culture"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the central disagreement?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether the sound works"},{"id":"o1","label":"How much guidance visitors need"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether objects exist"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reviewers differ over interpretive guidance, while agreeing on sound design.","passage":"The exhibition places everyday objects beside recordings of their owners' memories. One reviewer praises its intimate scale but finds the written labels too vague to explain the historical setting. Another welcomes that openness, arguing that visitors should form their own connections. Both admire the sound design. Their disagreement concerns how much interpretive guidance a museum should provide, rather than whether the objects are valuable or whether the recordings are technically effective.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What do both reviewers appreciate?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The sound design"},{"id":"o1","label":"Every written label"},{"id":"o2","label":"The lack of visitors"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The shared positive judgement concerns sound design.","passage":"The exhibition places everyday objects beside recordings of their owners' memories. One reviewer praises its intimate scale but finds the written labels too vague to explain the historical setting. Another welcomes that openness, arguing that visitors should form their own connections. Both admire the sound design. Their disagreement concerns how much interpretive guidance a museum should provide, rather than whether the objects are valuable or whether the recordings are technically effective.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the nuanced response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Could we separate the quality of the work from our preference for more guidance?"},{"id":"o1","label":"Every exhibition is equally good."},{"id":"o2","label":"Visitors should never ask questions."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response distinguishes a criterion or preference from a total verdict.","passage":"Friend: If the labels do not explain everything, the exhibition must be bad. You want to invite a more precise judgement.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"A review combines description, evaluation and reasons. An unfamiliar style is not automatically a flaw. Comparisons should identify the criterion used. Not only... but also links parallel elements. A mixed review can praise one aspect while criticising another; do not reduce it to simply positive or negative.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c1-stance","level":"C1","title":"Stance, hedging and calibrated claims","objective":"Express degrees of commitment and identify overstatement in complex argument.","project_prompt":"Write 250–350 words for a named audience on the unit issue. Organise a sustained argument, distinguish evidence from inference, and calibrate register and certainty.\nTopic: Stance, hedging and calibrated claims\nFocus: Express degrees of commitment and identify overstatement in complex argument.\nSource context: The committee's report cautiously endorses flexible working, noting improvements in retention across several participating firms. It stops short of attributing those improvements solely to location flexibility: the firms also changed management practices. Critics interpret the qualification as a lack of confidence in flexible work itself. Yet the report's position is narrower. It supports continued adoption where appropriate while resisting a universal causal claim. Its caution concerns the explanation and generalisability of the findings, not the existence of the observed retention changes.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Advanced argument calibrates commitment. The evidence is consistent with is weaker than the evidence establishes. Hedging is not evasiveness when it accurately reflects uncertainty. Fronted negative adverbials can trigger inversion: Rarely have we seen. Evaluate what a qualification limits: the population, the mechanism, the timescale or the certainty of the claim.","description":"Express degrees of commitment and identify overstatement in complex argument. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the qualification primarily about?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether any firm participated"},{"id":"o1","label":"Causation and generalisability"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether retention was observed at all"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The report accepts the observations but limits causal and universal interpretations.","passage":"The committee's report cautiously endorses flexible working, noting improvements in retention across several participating firms. It stops short of attributing those improvements solely to location flexibility: the firms also changed management practices. Critics interpret the qualification as a lack of confidence in flexible work itself. Yet the report's position is narrower. It supports continued adoption where appropriate while resisting a universal causal claim. Its caution concerns the explanation and generalisability of the findings, not the existence of the observed retention changes.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How do critics misread the report?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"They equate a limited causal claim with rejection of flexible work."},{"id":"o1","label":"They identify the management changes."},{"id":"o2","label":"They note that several firms participated."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"They turn methodological caution into broad opposition or doubt.","passage":"The committee's report cautiously endorses flexible working, noting improvements in retention across several participating firms. It stops short of attributing those improvements solely to location flexibility: the firms also changed management practices. Critics interpret the qualification as a lack of confidence in flexible work itself. Yet the report's position is narrower. It supports continued adoption where appropriate while resisting a universal causal claim. Its caution concerns the explanation and generalisability of the findings, not the existence of the observed retention changes.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Advanced argument calibrates commitment. The evidence is consistent with is weaker than the evidence establishes. Hedging is not evasiveness when it accurately reflects uncertainty. Fronted negative adverbials can trigger inversion: Rarely have we seen. Evaluate what a qualification limits: the population, the mechanism, the timescale or the certainty of the claim.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Rewrite with inversion, starting Rarely: We have rarely seen such consistent findings.","solution":"Rarely have we seen such consistent findings.","explanation":"Fronted rarely requires subject-auxiliary inversion.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: The findings are consistent ___ this explanation, but do not establish it.","solution":"with","explanation":"Consistent with expresses compatibility rather than proof.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Advanced argument calibrates commitment. The evidence is consistent with is weaker than the evidence establishes. Hedging is not evasiveness when it accurately reflects uncertainty. Fronted negative adverbials can trigger inversion: Rarely have we seen. Evaluate what a qualification limits: the population, the mechanism, the timescale or the certainty of the claim.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which objection is precise?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The revision changes a possible contribution into an unsupported guarantee."},{"id":"o1","label":"All confident writing is dishonest."},{"id":"o2","label":"The original phrase has no meaning."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The objection identifies the exact change in evidential commitment.","passage":"A colleague wants to replace 'may contribute to retention' with 'guarantees retention' in the report summary.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Advanced argument calibrates commitment. The evidence is consistent with is weaker than the evidence establishes. Hedging is not evasiveness when it accurately reflects uncertainty. Fronted negative adverbials can trigger inversion: Rarely have we seen. Evaluate what a qualification limits: the population, the mechanism, the timescale or the certainty of the claim.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the speaker doing?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Rejecting every result"},{"id":"o1","label":"Accepting a pattern while limiting generalisation"},{"id":"o2","label":"Claiming a universal law"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker neither dismisses nor universalises the pattern.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-stance"},{"prompt":"What would strengthen the case?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Repeating the slogan"},{"id":"o1","label":"A less transparent sample"},{"id":"o2","label":"Replication in less predictable settings"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"Replication would test whether the effect extends beyond the stable sample.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-stance"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the qualification primarily about?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether any firm participated"},{"id":"o1","label":"Causation and generalisability"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether retention was observed at all"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The report accepts the observations but limits causal and universal interpretations.","passage":"The committee's report cautiously endorses flexible working, noting improvements in retention across several participating firms. It stops short of attributing those improvements solely to location flexibility: the firms also changed management practices. Critics interpret the qualification as a lack of confidence in flexible work itself. Yet the report's position is narrower. It supports continued adoption where appropriate while resisting a universal causal claim. Its caution concerns the explanation and generalisability of the findings, not the existence of the observed retention changes.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How do critics misread the report?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"They equate a limited causal claim with rejection of flexible work."},{"id":"o1","label":"They identify the management changes."},{"id":"o2","label":"They note that several firms participated."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"They turn methodological caution into broad opposition or doubt.","passage":"The committee's report cautiously endorses flexible working, noting improvements in retention across several participating firms. It stops short of attributing those improvements solely to location flexibility: the firms also changed management practices. Critics interpret the qualification as a lack of confidence in flexible work itself. Yet the report's position is narrower. It supports continued adoption where appropriate while resisting a universal causal claim. Its caution concerns the explanation and generalisability of the findings, not the existence of the observed retention changes.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which objection is precise?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The revision changes a possible contribution into an unsupported guarantee."},{"id":"o1","label":"All confident writing is dishonest."},{"id":"o2","label":"The original phrase has no meaning."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The objection identifies the exact change in evidential commitment.","passage":"A colleague wants to replace 'may contribute to retention' with 'guarantees retention' in the report summary.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Advanced argument calibrates commitment. The evidence is consistent with is weaker than the evidence establishes. Hedging is not evasiveness when it accurately reflects uncertainty. Fronted negative adverbials can trigger inversion: Rarely have we seen. Evaluate what a qualification limits: the population, the mechanism, the timescale or the certainty of the claim.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c1-register","level":"C1","title":"Register and diplomatic disagreement","objective":"Adjust directness and register while preserving a substantive objection.","project_prompt":"Write 250–350 words for a named audience on the unit issue. Organise a sustained argument, distinguish evidence from inference, and calibrate register and certainty.\nTopic: Register and diplomatic disagreement\nFocus: Adjust directness and register while preserving a substantive objection.\nSource context: During a partnership meeting, the project lead says, 'The timetable is ambitious.' The supplier takes this as praise and confirms the launch date. In a follow-up message, the lead writes that the team cannot commit until testing is complete. The initial remark was intended as a reservation, but its indirectness left the practical consequence unstated. The revised message remains courteous while naming the dependency, the risk of premature launch and the earliest point at which a reliable date can be agreed.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Diplomatic language can reduce interpersonal friction without weakening a clear boundary. I am not persuaded that challenges an inference; with respect may sound confrontational if followed by dismissal. Match register to audience, power relationship and purpose. Formal concessive structures such as while and notwithstanding organise disagreement without pretending agreement.","description":"Adjust directness and register while preserving a substantive objection. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did the initial communication fail?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It named the testing dependency too clearly."},{"id":"o1","label":"The reservation was indirect and its consequence unstated."},{"id":"o2","label":"The supplier refused to attend."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Ambitious was interpreted positively because the intended reservation was not explicit.","passage":"During a partnership meeting, the project lead says, 'The timetable is ambitious.' The supplier takes this as praise and confirms the launch date. In a follow-up message, the lead writes that the team cannot commit until testing is complete. The initial remark was intended as a reservation, but its indirectness left the practical consequence unstated. The revised message remains courteous while naming the dependency, the risk of premature launch and the earliest point at which a reliable date can be agreed.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What improves the follow-up?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A courteous but explicit condition for commitment"},{"id":"o1","label":"A stronger insult"},{"id":"o2","label":"An unconditional confirmation"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The follow-up connects the concern to testing and the date decision.","passage":"During a partnership meeting, the project lead says, 'The timetable is ambitious.' The supplier takes this as praise and confirms the launch date. In a follow-up message, the lead writes that the team cannot commit until testing is complete. The initial remark was intended as a reservation, but its indirectness left the practical consequence unstated. The revised message remains courteous while naming the dependency, the risk of premature launch and the earliest point at which a reliable date can be agreed.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Diplomatic language can reduce interpersonal friction without weakening a clear boundary. I am not persuaded that challenges an inference; with respect may sound confrontational if followed by dismissal. Match register to audience, power relationship and purpose. Formal concessive structures such as while and notwithstanding organise disagreement without pretending agreement.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the formal concession: ___ we appreciate the urgency, testing must be completed. Use While or Because.","solution":"While","explanation":"While concedes the urgency without making it the reason to skip testing.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Rewrite more formally with the supplied words: We cannot agree yet. Begin: We are not yet in a position...","solution":"We are not yet in a position to agree.","explanation":"In a position to expresses present capacity in a formal register.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Diplomatic language can reduce interpersonal friction without weakening a clear boundary. I am not persuaded that challenges an inference; with respect may sound confrontational if followed by dismissal. Match register to audience, power relationship and purpose. Formal concessive structures such as while and notwithstanding organise disagreement without pretending agreement.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the effective reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Interesting. I am sure it will be fine."},{"id":"o1","label":"I appreciate the deadline, but I cannot approve publication until the figures are verified."},{"id":"o2","label":"Your proposal is ridiculous."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply combines acknowledgement, a clear boundary and its condition.","passage":"A senior colleague proposes publishing unverified figures today. You need to disagree respectfully and make the constraint explicit.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Diplomatic language can reduce interpersonal friction without weakening a clear boundary. I am not persuaded that challenges an inference; with respect may sound confrontational if followed by dismissal. Match register to audience, power relationship and purpose. Formal concessive structures such as while and notwithstanding organise disagreement without pretending agreement.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the main criticism?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Lack of material"},{"id":"o1","label":"Poor accessibility of the decision"},{"id":"o2","label":"Too few appendices"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The concern is finding the decision, not document length alone.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-register"},{"prompt":"What does 'certainly comprehensive' do here?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Acknowledges a strength before a reservation"},{"id":"o1","label":"Requests more detail everywhere"},{"id":"o2","label":"Confirms unconditional approval"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"It concedes breadth before introducing a functional criticism.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-register"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did the initial communication fail?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It named the testing dependency too clearly."},{"id":"o1","label":"The reservation was indirect and its consequence unstated."},{"id":"o2","label":"The supplier refused to attend."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Ambitious was interpreted positively because the intended reservation was not explicit.","passage":"During a partnership meeting, the project lead says, 'The timetable is ambitious.' The supplier takes this as praise and confirms the launch date. In a follow-up message, the lead writes that the team cannot commit until testing is complete. The initial remark was intended as a reservation, but its indirectness left the practical consequence unstated. The revised message remains courteous while naming the dependency, the risk of premature launch and the earliest point at which a reliable date can be agreed.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What improves the follow-up?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A courteous but explicit condition for commitment"},{"id":"o1","label":"A stronger insult"},{"id":"o2","label":"An unconditional confirmation"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The follow-up connects the concern to testing and the date decision.","passage":"During a partnership meeting, the project lead says, 'The timetable is ambitious.' The supplier takes this as praise and confirms the launch date. In a follow-up message, the lead writes that the team cannot commit until testing is complete. The initial remark was intended as a reservation, but its indirectness left the practical consequence unstated. The revised message remains courteous while naming the dependency, the risk of premature launch and the earliest point at which a reliable date can be agreed.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the effective reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Interesting. I am sure it will be fine."},{"id":"o1","label":"I appreciate the deadline, but I cannot approve publication until the figures are verified."},{"id":"o2","label":"Your proposal is ridiculous."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply combines acknowledgement, a clear boundary and its condition.","passage":"A senior colleague proposes publishing unverified figures today. You need to disagree respectfully and make the constraint explicit.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Diplomatic language can reduce interpersonal friction without weakening a clear boundary. I am not persuaded that challenges an inference; with respect may sound confrontational if followed by dismissal. Match register to audience, power relationship and purpose. Formal concessive structures such as while and notwithstanding organise disagreement without pretending agreement.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c1-synthesis","level":"C1","title":"Synthesising sources without flattening them","objective":"Combine perspectives while preserving disagreements and evidential limits.","project_prompt":"Write 250–350 words for a named audience on the unit issue. Organise a sustained argument, distinguish evidence from inference, and calibrate register and certainty.\nTopic: Synthesising sources without flattening them\nFocus: Combine perspectives while preserving disagreements and evidential limits.\nSource context: One study finds that remote employees report fewer interruptions; another finds that new recruits learn workplace routines more slowly when fully remote. These findings need not conflict. The first concerns established staff's concentration, whereas the second concerns newcomers' informal learning. A synthesis that simply labels remote work beneficial or harmful erases this distinction. A more useful account asks which tasks and career stages benefit from which arrangements, while acknowledging that self-reports and observed induction outcomes measure different things.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Synthesis organises sources around an issue, not just one source after another. Preserve differences in scope and method. Both texts agree that does not mean they share a solution. Nominalisation can compress an idea, but excessive abstraction hides agents and processes. Identify which apparent contradictions disappear when definitions or populations are distinguished.","description":"Combine perspectives while preserving disagreements and evidential limits. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why need the findings not conflict?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"They concern different groups and outcomes."},{"id":"o1","label":"One source must be fabricated."},{"id":"o2","label":"Both measure identical things."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Established staff's interruptions and newcomers' learning are different outcomes.","passage":"One study finds that remote employees report fewer interruptions; another finds that new recruits learn workplace routines more slowly when fully remote. These findings need not conflict. The first concerns established staff's concentration, whereas the second concerns newcomers' informal learning. A synthesis that simply labels remote work beneficial or harmful erases this distinction. A more useful account asks which tasks and career stages benefit from which arrangements, while acknowledging that self-reports and observed induction outcomes measure different things.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which synthesis is most faithful?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Remote work is uniformly harmful."},{"id":"o1","label":"Its effects may depend on task and career stage."},{"id":"o2","label":"Both sources prove the same mechanism."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The proposed synthesis retains conditions and differences between methods.","passage":"One study finds that remote employees report fewer interruptions; another finds that new recruits learn workplace routines more slowly when fully remote. These findings need not conflict. The first concerns established staff's concentration, whereas the second concerns newcomers' informal learning. A synthesis that simply labels remote work beneficial or harmful erases this distinction. A more useful account asks which tasks and career stages benefit from which arrangements, while acknowledging that self-reports and observed induction outcomes measure different things.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Synthesis organises sources around an issue, not just one source after another. Preserve differences in scope and method. Both texts agree that does not mean they share a solution. Nominalisation can compress an idea, but excessive abstraction hides agents and processes. Identify which apparent contradictions disappear when definitions or populations are distinguished.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Nominalise improve in this sentence: The ___ of induction procedures is a priority.","solution":"improvement","explanation":"Improvement names the process as a noun.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete the contrast: The first study concerns concentration, ___ the second concerns induction. Use whereas.","solution":"whereas","explanation":"Whereas links the contrasting scopes.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Synthesis organises sources around an issue, not just one source after another. Preserve differences in scope and method. Both texts agree that does not mean they share a solution. Nominalisation can compress an idea, but excessive abstraction hides agents and processes. Identify which apparent contradictions disappear when definitions or populations are distinguished.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the better summary.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The studies examine different outcomes, suggesting arrangements should reflect staff experience and tasks."},{"id":"o1","label":"Experts can never agree on anything."},{"id":"o2","label":"The two studies cancel each other out."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The summary relates the findings without inventing a direct contradiction.","passage":"An editor asks you to compress both studies into: 'Experts disagree about whether remote work works.' You want to preserve the meaningful distinction.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Synthesis organises sources around an issue, not just one source after another. Preserve differences in scope and method. Both texts agree that does not mean they share a solution. Nominalisation can compress an idea, but excessive abstraction hides agents and processes. Identify which apparent contradictions disappear when definitions or populations are distinguished.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Where does the apparent agreement conceal a difference?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"In the meaning of access"},{"id":"o1","label":"In the publication language"},{"id":"o2","label":"In the number of authors"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The same word refers to entry in one account and meaningful participation in another.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-synthesis"},{"prompt":"What can increased enrolment establish by itself?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Meaningful participation for everyone"},{"id":"o1","label":"More entries, not necessarily meaningful participation"},{"id":"o2","label":"No change in either measure"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The first measure cannot by itself answer the second question.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-synthesis"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why need the findings not conflict?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"They concern different groups and outcomes."},{"id":"o1","label":"One source must be fabricated."},{"id":"o2","label":"Both measure identical things."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Established staff's interruptions and newcomers' learning are different outcomes.","passage":"One study finds that remote employees report fewer interruptions; another finds that new recruits learn workplace routines more slowly when fully remote. These findings need not conflict. The first concerns established staff's concentration, whereas the second concerns newcomers' informal learning. A synthesis that simply labels remote work beneficial or harmful erases this distinction. A more useful account asks which tasks and career stages benefit from which arrangements, while acknowledging that self-reports and observed induction outcomes measure different things.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which synthesis is most faithful?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Remote work is uniformly harmful."},{"id":"o1","label":"Its effects may depend on task and career stage."},{"id":"o2","label":"Both sources prove the same mechanism."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The proposed synthesis retains conditions and differences between methods.","passage":"One study finds that remote employees report fewer interruptions; another finds that new recruits learn workplace routines more slowly when fully remote. These findings need not conflict. The first concerns established staff's concentration, whereas the second concerns newcomers' informal learning. A synthesis that simply labels remote work beneficial or harmful erases this distinction. A more useful account asks which tasks and career stages benefit from which arrangements, while acknowledging that self-reports and observed induction outcomes measure different things.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the better summary.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The studies examine different outcomes, suggesting arrangements should reflect staff experience and tasks."},{"id":"o1","label":"Experts can never agree on anything."},{"id":"o2","label":"The two studies cancel each other out."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The summary relates the findings without inventing a direct contradiction.","passage":"An editor asks you to compress both studies into: 'Experts disagree about whether remote work works.' You want to preserve the meaningful distinction.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Synthesis organises sources around an issue, not just one source after another. Preserve differences in scope and method. Both texts agree that does not mean they share a solution. Nominalisation can compress an idea, but excessive abstraction hides agents and processes. Identify which apparent contradictions disappear when definitions or populations are distinguished.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c1-implication","level":"C1","title":"Implication and institutional language","objective":"Infer pragmatic meaning without treating an inference as explicit fact.","project_prompt":"Write 250–350 words for a named audience on the unit issue. Organise a sustained argument, distinguish evidence from inference, and calibrate register and certainty.\nTopic: Implication and institutional language\nFocus: Infer pragmatic meaning without treating an inference as explicit fact.\nSource context: The announcement states that 'implementation difficulties were encountered' and that 'lessons will inform future practice'. It identifies neither the decisions that created the difficulties nor the person responsible for acting on the lessons. Readers might reasonably infer an attempt to avoid blame, but that motive is not established by the wording alone. What can be demonstrated is the absence of named accountability, a timetable and a measurable commitment. A careful critique distinguishes those textual features from speculation about the authors' intentions.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: An implication is suggested rather than stated. Inferences depend on context and remain revisable. Institutional language may distribute or obscure responsibility through passive voice, agentless nominalisations and vague references. Ask what is explicitly committed to, what is merely envisaged and who can be held responsible for the next step.","description":"Infer pragmatic meaning without treating an inference as explicit fact. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which claim is demonstrable from the wording?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The authors deliberately lied."},{"id":"o1","label":"Named accountability and a timetable are absent."},{"id":"o2","label":"All difficulties were unavoidable."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Those omissions are observable; motives are not established.","passage":"The announcement states that 'implementation difficulties were encountered' and that 'lessons will inform future practice'. It identifies neither the decisions that created the difficulties nor the person responsible for acting on the lessons. Readers might reasonably infer an attempt to avoid blame, but that motive is not established by the wording alone. What can be demonstrated is the absence of named accountability, a timetable and a measurable commitment. A careful critique distinguishes those textual features from speculation about the authors' intentions.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What distinction does the passage insist on?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Textual evidence versus speculation about intention"},{"id":"o1","label":"Written versus spoken English only"},{"id":"o2","label":"Lessons versus timetables as synonyms"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"A plausible motive remains an inference rather than an explicit fact.","passage":"The announcement states that 'implementation difficulties were encountered' and that 'lessons will inform future practice'. It identifies neither the decisions that created the difficulties nor the person responsible for acting on the lessons. Readers might reasonably infer an attempt to avoid blame, but that motive is not established by the wording alone. What can be demonstrated is the absence of named accountability, a timetable and a measurable commitment. A careful critique distinguishes those textual features from speculation about the authors' intentions.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"An implication is suggested rather than stated. Inferences depend on context and remain revisable. Institutional language may distribute or obscure responsibility through passive voice, agentless nominalisations and vague references. Ask what is explicitly committed to, what is merely envisaged and who can be held responsible for the next step.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Make responsibility explicit: A review will be conducted by the board. Begin: The board...","solution":"The board will conduct a review.","explanation":"Active voice names the board as the responsible agent.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: The wording gives rise ___ concerns about accountability.","solution":"to","explanation":"Give rise to means cause or generate.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"An implication is suggested rather than stated. Inferences depend on context and remain revisable. Institutional language may distribute or obscure responsibility through passive voice, agentless nominalisations and vague references. Ask what is explicitly committed to, what is merely envisaged and who can be held responsible for the next step.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the careful response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"We can show that accountability is unspecified; deliberate deception would require further evidence."},{"id":"o1","label":"There is nothing to criticise."},{"id":"o2","label":"Vagueness always proves fraud."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply separates observable omission from an unsupported intentional claim.","passage":"Colleague: The vague announcement proves the authors wanted to deceive us. You want to criticise the text without overstating knowledge of motive.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"An implication is suggested rather than stated. Inferences depend on context and remain revisable. Institutional language may distribute or obscure responsibility through passive voice, agentless nominalisations and vague references. Ask what is explicitly committed to, what is merely envisaged and who can be held responsible for the next step.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What commitment has actually been made?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Publication by December"},{"id":"o1","label":"An unspecified intention to review"},{"id":"o2","label":"A signed publication deadline"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Envisage and in due course do not fix a publication date.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-implication"},{"prompt":"What should be added if timing matters?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A more enthusiastic adjective"},{"id":"o1","label":"A named deadline"},{"id":"o2","label":"A less explicit agreement"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"An explicit deadline avoids relying on inference.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-implication"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Which claim is demonstrable from the wording?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The authors deliberately lied."},{"id":"o1","label":"Named accountability and a timetable are absent."},{"id":"o2","label":"All difficulties were unavoidable."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Those omissions are observable; motives are not established.","passage":"The announcement states that 'implementation difficulties were encountered' and that 'lessons will inform future practice'. It identifies neither the decisions that created the difficulties nor the person responsible for acting on the lessons. Readers might reasonably infer an attempt to avoid blame, but that motive is not established by the wording alone. What can be demonstrated is the absence of named accountability, a timetable and a measurable commitment. A careful critique distinguishes those textual features from speculation about the authors' intentions.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What distinction does the passage insist on?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Textual evidence versus speculation about intention"},{"id":"o1","label":"Written versus spoken English only"},{"id":"o2","label":"Lessons versus timetables as synonyms"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"A plausible motive remains an inference rather than an explicit fact.","passage":"The announcement states that 'implementation difficulties were encountered' and that 'lessons will inform future practice'. It identifies neither the decisions that created the difficulties nor the person responsible for acting on the lessons. Readers might reasonably infer an attempt to avoid blame, but that motive is not established by the wording alone. What can be demonstrated is the absence of named accountability, a timetable and a measurable commitment. A careful critique distinguishes those textual features from speculation about the authors' intentions.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the careful response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"We can show that accountability is unspecified; deliberate deception would require further evidence."},{"id":"o1","label":"There is nothing to criticise."},{"id":"o2","label":"Vagueness always proves fraud."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply separates observable omission from an unsupported intentional claim.","passage":"Colleague: The vague announcement proves the authors wanted to deceive us. You want to criticise the text without overstating knowledge of motive.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"An implication is suggested rather than stated. Inferences depend on context and remain revisable. Institutional language may distribute or obscure responsibility through passive voice, agentless nominalisations and vague references. Ask what is explicitly committed to, what is merely envisaged and who can be held responsible for the next step.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c1-evaluation","level":"C1","title":"Evaluation and counterarguments","objective":"Assess a counterargument and distinguish necessary from sufficient conditions.","project_prompt":"Write 250–350 words for a named audience on the unit issue. Organise a sustained argument, distinguish evidence from inference, and calibrate register and certainty.\nTopic: Evaluation and counterarguments\nFocus: Assess a counterargument and distinguish necessary from sufficient conditions.\nSource context: A funding proposal argues that public data access will automatically improve research quality. A reviewer agrees that access removes a barrier but notes that data also need usable documentation, appropriate methods and ethical safeguards. This is not an argument against openness. It challenges the claim that openness alone is sufficient. Pointing to one poorly documented open dataset would undermine an automatic guarantee, but would not establish that closed datasets are generally better. The revised proposal makes access one component of a broader quality strategy.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: A necessary condition must hold for an outcome, but may not guarantee it. A sufficient condition is enough to produce the outcome under stated assumptions. A counterexample can refute a universal claim without proving the opposite universal claim. Address the strongest relevant objection, not an easier caricature of it.","description":"Assess a counterargument and distinguish necessary from sufficient conditions. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the reviewer challenge?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"All access to public data"},{"id":"o1","label":"The sufficiency of access alone"},{"id":"o2","label":"The existence of research methods"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reviewer accepts a benefit while rejecting an automatic guarantee.","passage":"A funding proposal argues that public data access will automatically improve research quality. A reviewer agrees that access removes a barrier but notes that data also need usable documentation, appropriate methods and ethical safeguards. This is not an argument against openness. It challenges the claim that openness alone is sufficient. Pointing to one poorly documented open dataset would undermine an automatic guarantee, but would not establish that closed datasets are generally better. The revised proposal makes access one component of a broader quality strategy.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What would one poor open dataset establish?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"That all closed data are better"},{"id":"o1","label":"That openness never helps"},{"id":"o2","label":"A counterexample to the automatic guarantee"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"A counterexample defeats a universal guarantee, not every weaker benefit claim.","passage":"A funding proposal argues that public data access will automatically improve research quality. A reviewer agrees that access removes a barrier but notes that data also need usable documentation, appropriate methods and ethical safeguards. This is not an argument against openness. It challenges the claim that openness alone is sufficient. Pointing to one poorly documented open dataset would undermine an automatic guarantee, but would not establish that closed datasets are generally better. The revised proposal makes access one component of a broader quality strategy.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A necessary condition must hold for an outcome, but may not guarantee it. A sufficient condition is enough to produce the outcome under stated assumptions. A counterexample can refute a universal claim without proving the opposite universal claim. Address the strongest relevant objection, not an easier caricature of it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: Access is necessary but not ___ for quality. Use sufficient.","solution":"sufficient","explanation":"Necessary does not entail sufficient.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Rewrite using by no means and inversion: This is not a complete solution. Begin: By no means...","solution":"By no means is this a complete solution.","explanation":"A fronted negative phrase triggers inversion.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"A necessary condition must hold for an outcome, but may not guarantee it. A sufficient condition is enough to produce the outcome under stated assumptions. A counterexample can refute a universal claim without proving the opposite universal claim. Address the strongest relevant objection, not an easier caricature of it.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the precise reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"I support access; my objection is to treating it as sufficient on its own."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, no data should ever be shared."},{"id":"o2","label":"Quality cannot be discussed."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply separates support for a component from acceptance of a guarantee.","passage":"Presenter: You questioned whether access guarantees quality, so you must oppose access. You want to correct the characterisation.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A necessary condition must hold for an outcome, but may not guarantee it. A sufficient condition is enough to produce the outcome under stated assumptions. A counterexample can refute a universal claim without proving the opposite universal claim. Address the strongest relevant objection, not an easier caricature of it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the objection's focus?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"An unprovided dependency"},{"id":"o1","label":"A proof of impossibility"},{"id":"o2","label":"Opposition to all savings"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The staffing dependency is missing from the budget.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-evaluation"},{"prompt":"How should the savings be described?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Guaranteed"},{"id":"o1","label":"Conditional"},{"id":"o2","label":"Already achieved"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker explicitly calls them conditional.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-evaluation"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the reviewer challenge?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"All access to public data"},{"id":"o1","label":"The sufficiency of access alone"},{"id":"o2","label":"The existence of research methods"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reviewer accepts a benefit while rejecting an automatic guarantee.","passage":"A funding proposal argues that public data access will automatically improve research quality. A reviewer agrees that access removes a barrier but notes that data also need usable documentation, appropriate methods and ethical safeguards. This is not an argument against openness. It challenges the claim that openness alone is sufficient. Pointing to one poorly documented open dataset would undermine an automatic guarantee, but would not establish that closed datasets are generally better. The revised proposal makes access one component of a broader quality strategy.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What would one poor open dataset establish?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"That all closed data are better"},{"id":"o1","label":"That openness never helps"},{"id":"o2","label":"A counterexample to the automatic guarantee"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"A counterexample defeats a universal guarantee, not every weaker benefit claim.","passage":"A funding proposal argues that public data access will automatically improve research quality. A reviewer agrees that access removes a barrier but notes that data also need usable documentation, appropriate methods and ethical safeguards. This is not an argument against openness. It challenges the claim that openness alone is sufficient. Pointing to one poorly documented open dataset would undermine an automatic guarantee, but would not establish that closed datasets are generally better. The revised proposal makes access one component of a broader quality strategy.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the precise reply.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"I support access; my objection is to treating it as sufficient on its own."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, no data should ever be shared."},{"id":"o2","label":"Quality cannot be discussed."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply separates support for a component from acceptance of a guarantee.","passage":"Presenter: You questioned whether access guarantees quality, so you must oppose access. You want to correct the characterisation.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"A necessary condition must hold for an outcome, but may not guarantee it. A sufficient condition is enough to produce the outcome under stated assumptions. A counterexample can refute a universal claim without proving the opposite universal claim. Address the strongest relevant objection, not an easier caricature of it.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c1-mediation","level":"C1","title":"Mediating technical information","objective":"Reformulate specialist information for a non-specialist without losing conditions.","project_prompt":"Write 250–350 words for a named audience on the unit issue. Organise a sustained argument, distinguish evidence from inference, and calibrate register and certainty.\nTopic: Mediating technical information\nFocus: Reformulate specialist information for a non-specialist without losing conditions.\nSource context: A technical note says a monitoring device estimates average exposure over a day, not the maximum concentration at every instant. A school administrator asks whether one low daily reading proves there were no short peaks. An accurate plain-language explanation must say no: an average can conceal brief higher values. It should not imply the device is useless, because daily exposure remains relevant to a different question. The recommendation is to match the measurement method to the decision, and use time-resolved data when short peaks matter.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Mediation is not word-for-word replacement. Identify the audience's decision, retain the conditions that affect it and explain essential terms. Omit incidental detail rather than material uncertainty. In mixed conditionals, an unreal past cause can have a present result: If we had checked, we would know now.","description":"Reformulate specialist information for a non-specialist without losing conditions. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the low daily average fail to rule out?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Any valid measurement"},{"id":"o1","label":"Brief higher concentrations"},{"id":"o2","label":"The existence of a whole day"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"An average does not identify every momentary peak.","passage":"A technical note says a monitoring device estimates average exposure over a day, not the maximum concentration at every instant. A school administrator asks whether one low daily reading proves there were no short peaks. An accurate plain-language explanation must say no: an average can conceal brief higher values. It should not imply the device is useless, because daily exposure remains relevant to a different question. The recommendation is to match the measurement method to the decision, and use time-resolved data when short peaks matter.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is the appropriate recommendation?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Discard every device"},{"id":"o1","label":"Treat all measurements as interchangeable"},{"id":"o2","label":"Choose data suited to the decision"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The daily average and time-resolved data answer different questions.","passage":"A technical note says a monitoring device estimates average exposure over a day, not the maximum concentration at every instant. A school administrator asks whether one low daily reading proves there were no short peaks. An accurate plain-language explanation must say no: an average can conceal brief higher values. It should not imply the device is useless, because daily exposure remains relevant to a different question. The recommendation is to match the measurement method to the decision, and use time-resolved data when short peaks matter.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Mediation is not word-for-word replacement. Identify the audience's decision, retain the conditions that affect it and explain essential terms. Omit incidental detail rather than material uncertainty. In mixed conditionals, an unreal past cause can have a present result: If we had checked, we would know now.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the mixed conditional: If we had measured the peaks, we ___ know more now. Use would.","solution":"would","explanation":"Would plus base form gives the present result of a counterfactual past.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: The method should be suited ___ the question being asked.","solution":"to","explanation":"Suited to expresses fitness for a purpose.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Mediation is not word-for-word replacement. Identify the audience's decision, retain the conditions that affect it and explain essential terms. Omit incidental detail rather than material uncertainty. In mixed conditionals, an unreal past cause can have a present result: If we had checked, we would know now.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the accurate reformulation.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"No. The average describes the whole day; brief peaks require more detailed measurements."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes. An average is always the maximum."},{"id":"o2","label":"No measurement can tell us anything."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply preserves the limitation without dismissing all measurement.","passage":"Administrator: Can I tell parents that the low average means there were no peaks? You must give a clear, non-alarmist explanation.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Mediation is not word-for-word replacement. Identify the audience's decision, retain the conditions that affect it and explain essential terms. Omit incidental detail rather than material uncertainty. In mixed conditionals, an unreal past cause can have a present result: If we had checked, we would know now.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What metaphor is being rejected?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A forecast as an exact future appointment"},{"id":"o1","label":"Planning as a useful activity"},{"id":"o2","label":"A range as several possibilities"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The metaphor highlights the mistaken demand for a precise guarantee.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-mediation"},{"prompt":"How should the range be used?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Ignore all but its midpoint"},{"id":"o1","label":"Test decisions across plausible outcomes"},{"id":"o2","label":"Promise the most convenient outcome"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker recommends robustness across the range.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c1-mediation"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the low daily average fail to rule out?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Any valid measurement"},{"id":"o1","label":"Brief higher concentrations"},{"id":"o2","label":"The existence of a whole day"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"An average does not identify every momentary peak.","passage":"A technical note says a monitoring device estimates average exposure over a day, not the maximum concentration at every instant. A school administrator asks whether one low daily reading proves there were no short peaks. An accurate plain-language explanation must say no: an average can conceal brief higher values. It should not imply the device is useless, because daily exposure remains relevant to a different question. The recommendation is to match the measurement method to the decision, and use time-resolved data when short peaks matter.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is the appropriate recommendation?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Discard every device"},{"id":"o1","label":"Treat all measurements as interchangeable"},{"id":"o2","label":"Choose data suited to the decision"}],"solution":"o2","explanation":"The daily average and time-resolved data answer different questions.","passage":"A technical note says a monitoring device estimates average exposure over a day, not the maximum concentration at every instant. A school administrator asks whether one low daily reading proves there were no short peaks. An accurate plain-language explanation must say no: an average can conceal brief higher values. It should not imply the device is useless, because daily exposure remains relevant to a different question. The recommendation is to match the measurement method to the decision, and use time-resolved data when short peaks matter.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the accurate reformulation.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"No. The average describes the whole day; brief peaks require more detailed measurements."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes. An average is always the maximum."},{"id":"o2","label":"No measurement can tell us anything."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The reply preserves the limitation without dismissing all measurement.","passage":"Administrator: Can I tell parents that the low average means there were no peaks? You must give a clear, non-alarmist explanation.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Mediation is not word-for-word replacement. Identify the audience's decision, retain the conditions that affect it and explain essential terms. Omit incidental detail rather than material uncertainty. In mixed conditionals, an unreal past cause can have a present result: If we had checked, we would know now.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c2-rhetoric","level":"C2","title":"Rhetorical framing and presupposition","objective":"Analyse how framing invites acceptance of an unestablished premise.","project_prompt":"Write 300–450 words critically reformulating the unit argument for a different audience. Preserve its qualifications, analyse an alternative reading, and justify precise lexical and rhetorical choices.\nTopic: Rhetorical framing and presupposition\nFocus: Analyse how framing invites acceptance of an unestablished premise.\nSource context: The editorial asks when the city will 'finally admit' that its experiment has failed. The phrasing presents failure as settled and casts disagreement as reluctance to confess. Yet the evidence described is mixed: one target was missed, another was exceeded, and the principal outcome has not been measured. A response that merely answers 'next year' accepts the frame. A more discriminating response first questions the criterion of failure, then evaluates each outcome separately. This does not vindicate the experiment; it prevents a rhetorical premise from doing the work of an argument.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: A loaded question can presuppose the very proposition under dispute. Asking when a policy's benefits will finally be acknowledged presupposes benefits and a failure to acknowledge them. Distinguish grammatical presupposition, contextual implication and independently warranted conclusions. Rejecting a loaded premise does not require asserting its opposite.","description":"Analyse how framing invites acceptance of an unestablished premise. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What does 'finally admit' invite the reader to accept?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"That failure is already established"},{"id":"o1","label":"That no experiment occurred"},{"id":"o2","label":"That every target was exceeded"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Admit frames failure as a truth awaiting reluctant recognition.","passage":"The editorial asks when the city will 'finally admit' that its experiment has failed. The phrasing presents failure as settled and casts disagreement as reluctance to confess. Yet the evidence described is mixed: one target was missed, another was exceeded, and the principal outcome has not been measured. A response that merely answers 'next year' accepts the frame. A more discriminating response first questions the criterion of failure, then evaluates each outcome separately. This does not vindicate the experiment; it prevents a rhetorical premise from doing the work of an argument.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What would questioning the frame establish by itself?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The experiment succeeded completely."},{"id":"o1","label":"The failure claim still needs an argued criterion."},{"id":"o2","label":"No outcome can be measured."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Challenging a premise does not prove the opposite conclusion.","passage":"The editorial asks when the city will 'finally admit' that its experiment has failed. The phrasing presents failure as settled and casts disagreement as reluctance to confess. Yet the evidence described is mixed: one target was missed, another was exceeded, and the principal outcome has not been measured. A response that merely answers 'next year' accepts the frame. A more discriminating response first questions the criterion of failure, then evaluates each outcome separately. This does not vindicate the experiment; it prevents a rhetorical premise from doing the work of an argument.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A loaded question can presuppose the very proposition under dispute. Asking when a policy's benefits will finally be acknowledged presupposes benefits and a failure to acknowledge them. Distinguish grammatical presupposition, contextual implication and independently warranted conclusions. Rejecting a loaded premise does not require asserting its opposite.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Rewrite with emphatic what-cleft: We need an explicit criterion. Begin: What we need...","solution":"What we need is an explicit criterion.","explanation":"The what-cleft foregrounds the missing criterion.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete the correlative: The issue is not ___ the policy is popular, but whether it meets its stated aims.","solution":"whether","explanation":"Not whether... but whether contrasts the questions under consideration.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"A loaded question can presuppose the very proposition under dispute. Asking when a policy's benefits will finally be acknowledged presupposes benefits and a failure to acknowledge them. Distinguish grammatical presupposition, contextual implication and independently warranted conclusions. Rejecting a loaded premise does not require asserting its opposite.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the rigorous response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Next Tuesday."},{"id":"o1","label":"Let us specify the criteria: some targets were missed, others met, and the main outcome is unmeasured."},{"id":"o2","label":"All criticism is illegitimate."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply replaces the presupposition with differentiated evaluation.","passage":"Interviewer: When will you finally concede that the trial was a disaster? You need to resist the premise without avoiding evaluation.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"A loaded question can presuppose the very proposition under dispute. Asking when a policy's benefits will finally be acknowledged presupposes benefits and a failure to acknowledge them. Distinguish grammatical presupposition, contextual implication and independently warranted conclusions. Rejecting a loaded premise does not require asserting its opposite.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the speaker criticising?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Any public payment"},{"id":"o1","label":"A label substituting for justification"},{"id":"o2","label":"The arithmetic of a stated cost"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The objection concerns rhetorical framing doing the work of justification.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-rhetoric"},{"prompt":"What contrast does the label create?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Different factual amounts"},{"id":"o1","label":"A different story about deservingness"},{"id":"o2","label":"A new recipient by legal definition"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker emphasises the label's narrative effect, not a change in cost.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-rhetoric"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What does 'finally admit' invite the reader to accept?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"That failure is already established"},{"id":"o1","label":"That no experiment occurred"},{"id":"o2","label":"That every target was exceeded"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Admit frames failure as a truth awaiting reluctant recognition.","passage":"The editorial asks when the city will 'finally admit' that its experiment has failed. The phrasing presents failure as settled and casts disagreement as reluctance to confess. Yet the evidence described is mixed: one target was missed, another was exceeded, and the principal outcome has not been measured. A response that merely answers 'next year' accepts the frame. A more discriminating response first questions the criterion of failure, then evaluates each outcome separately. This does not vindicate the experiment; it prevents a rhetorical premise from doing the work of an argument.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What would questioning the frame establish by itself?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The experiment succeeded completely."},{"id":"o1","label":"The failure claim still needs an argued criterion."},{"id":"o2","label":"No outcome can be measured."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Challenging a premise does not prove the opposite conclusion.","passage":"The editorial asks when the city will 'finally admit' that its experiment has failed. The phrasing presents failure as settled and casts disagreement as reluctance to confess. Yet the evidence described is mixed: one target was missed, another was exceeded, and the principal outcome has not been measured. A response that merely answers 'next year' accepts the frame. A more discriminating response first questions the criterion of failure, then evaluates each outcome separately. This does not vindicate the experiment; it prevents a rhetorical premise from doing the work of an argument.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the rigorous response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Next Tuesday."},{"id":"o1","label":"Let us specify the criteria: some targets were missed, others met, and the main outcome is unmeasured."},{"id":"o2","label":"All criticism is illegitimate."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The reply replaces the presupposition with differentiated evaluation.","passage":"Interviewer: When will you finally concede that the trial was a disaster? You need to resist the premise without avoiding evaluation.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"A loaded question can presuppose the very proposition under dispute. Asking when a policy's benefits will finally be acknowledged presupposes benefits and a failure to acknowledge them. Distinguish grammatical presupposition, contextual implication and independently warranted conclusions. Rejecting a loaded premise does not require asserting its opposite.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c2-irony","level":"C2","title":"Irony, understatement and perspective","objective":"Infer irony from context while keeping literal and intended meanings distinct.","project_prompt":"Write 300–450 words critically reformulating the unit argument for a different audience. Preserve its qualifications, analyse an alternative reading, and justify precise lexical and rhetorical choices.\nTopic: Irony, understatement and perspective\nFocus: Infer irony from context while keeping literal and intended meanings distinct.\nSource context: After the committee's third meeting about arranging a meeting, Lina remarked that their efficiency was 'a model for the ages'. The narrator adds that no agenda had yet been agreed. The extravagant praise clashes with the procedural stagnation, supporting an ironic reading. Another member's later comment, 'We may be slightly behind', is an understatement of the same delay. Neither remark supplies a plan. The passage exposes institutional inertia through the distance between how progress is described and what has actually been accomplished, rather than through a direct accusation of incompetence.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Irony often creates a gap between literal praise and contextual criticism. Understatement deliberately expresses less than the situation warrants. Neither can be identified from a word alone: context, shared assumptions and delivery matter. Explain what evidence supports an ironic reading, and acknowledge alternatives when delivery or context is unavailable.","description":"Infer irony from context while keeping literal and intended meanings distinct. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What supports the ironic reading of Lina's remark?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The mismatch between extravagant praise and lack of progress"},{"id":"o1","label":"The word efficiency in isolation"},{"id":"o2","label":"A completed agenda"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Contextual stagnation conflicts with the apparent praise.","passage":"After the committee's third meeting about arranging a meeting, Lina remarked that their efficiency was 'a model for the ages'. The narrator adds that no agenda had yet been agreed. The extravagant praise clashes with the procedural stagnation, supporting an ironic reading. Another member's later comment, 'We may be slightly behind', is an understatement of the same delay. Neither remark supplies a plan. The passage exposes institutional inertia through the distance between how progress is described and what has actually been accomplished, rather than through a direct accusation of incompetence.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How does 'slightly behind' function?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"As an exact numerical estimate"},{"id":"o1","label":"As understatement of a substantial delay"},{"id":"o2","label":"As proof the meeting succeeded"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The mild expression downplays the delay already established.","passage":"After the committee's third meeting about arranging a meeting, Lina remarked that their efficiency was 'a model for the ages'. The narrator adds that no agenda had yet been agreed. The extravagant praise clashes with the procedural stagnation, supporting an ironic reading. Another member's later comment, 'We may be slightly behind', is an understatement of the same delay. Neither remark supplies a plan. The passage exposes institutional inertia through the distance between how progress is described and what has actually been accomplished, rather than through a direct accusation of incompetence.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Irony often creates a gap between literal praise and contextual criticism. Understatement deliberately expresses less than the situation warrants. Neither can be identified from a word alone: context, shared assumptions and delivery matter. Explain what evidence supports an ironic reading, and acknowledge alternatives when delivery or context is unavailable.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Rewrite with such...that: Their delay was so great that the launch was missed. Begin: Such was...","solution":"Such was their delay that the launch was missed.","explanation":"Such was fronting creates an emphatic result construction.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Complete: The praise is at odds ___ the events described.","solution":"with","explanation":"At odds with expresses conflict or inconsistency.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Irony often creates a gap between literal praise and contextual criticism. Understatement deliberately expresses less than the situation warrants. Neither can be identified from a word alone: context, shared assumptions and delivery matter. Explain what evidence supports an ironic reading, and acknowledge alternatives when delivery or context is unavailable.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the context-sensitive correction.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The surrounding lack of progress makes the apparent praise ironic, not straightforward endorsement."},{"id":"o1","label":"Any extravagant phrase is always ironic."},{"id":"o2","label":"Literal meanings never matter."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The correction grounds its interpretation in contextual evidence.","passage":"A colleague quotes 'a model for the ages' as sincere approval, omitting the repeated meetings and absent agenda.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Irony often creates a gap between literal praise and contextual criticism. Understatement deliberately expresses less than the situation warrants. Neither can be identified from a word alone: context, shared assumptions and delivery matter. Explain what evidence supports an ironic reading, and acknowledge alternatives when delivery or context is unavailable.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"How should 'wonderfully decisive' be understood?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Literal praise of a final decision"},{"id":"o1","label":"Ironic criticism of indecision"},{"id":"o2","label":"A neutral duration report"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The absence of any decision contradicts the literal praise.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-irony"},{"prompt":"What intensifies the criticism?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A detailed implementation plan"},{"id":"o1","label":"The recursive joke about postponement"},{"id":"o2","label":"A claim that the meeting was brief"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Postponing a postponement extends the joke about procedural inertia.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-irony"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What supports the ironic reading of Lina's remark?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The mismatch between extravagant praise and lack of progress"},{"id":"o1","label":"The word efficiency in isolation"},{"id":"o2","label":"A completed agenda"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Contextual stagnation conflicts with the apparent praise.","passage":"After the committee's third meeting about arranging a meeting, Lina remarked that their efficiency was 'a model for the ages'. The narrator adds that no agenda had yet been agreed. The extravagant praise clashes with the procedural stagnation, supporting an ironic reading. Another member's later comment, 'We may be slightly behind', is an understatement of the same delay. Neither remark supplies a plan. The passage exposes institutional inertia through the distance between how progress is described and what has actually been accomplished, rather than through a direct accusation of incompetence.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"How does 'slightly behind' function?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"As an exact numerical estimate"},{"id":"o1","label":"As understatement of a substantial delay"},{"id":"o2","label":"As proof the meeting succeeded"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The mild expression downplays the delay already established.","passage":"After the committee's third meeting about arranging a meeting, Lina remarked that their efficiency was 'a model for the ages'. The narrator adds that no agenda had yet been agreed. The extravagant praise clashes with the procedural stagnation, supporting an ironic reading. Another member's later comment, 'We may be slightly behind', is an understatement of the same delay. Neither remark supplies a plan. The passage exposes institutional inertia through the distance between how progress is described and what has actually been accomplished, rather than through a direct accusation of incompetence.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the context-sensitive correction.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The surrounding lack of progress makes the apparent praise ironic, not straightforward endorsement."},{"id":"o1","label":"Any extravagant phrase is always ironic."},{"id":"o2","label":"Literal meanings never matter."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The correction grounds its interpretation in contextual evidence.","passage":"A colleague quotes 'a model for the ages' as sincere approval, omitting the repeated meetings and absent agenda.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Irony often creates a gap between literal praise and contextual criticism. Understatement deliberately expresses less than the situation warrants. Neither can be identified from a word alone: context, shared assumptions and delivery matter. Explain what evidence supports an ironic reading, and acknowledge alternatives when delivery or context is unavailable.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c2-ambiguity","level":"C2","title":"Ambiguity and scope","objective":"Resolve syntactic and pragmatic ambiguity without inventing certainty.","project_prompt":"Write 300–450 words critically reformulating the unit argument for a different audience. Preserve its qualifications, analyse an alternative reading, and justify precise lexical and rhetorical choices.\nTopic: Ambiguity and scope\nFocus: Resolve syntactic and pragmatic ambiguity without inventing certainty.\nSource context: A contract summary says that the provider will not replace all damaged components. One manager reads this as a refusal to replace any; another assumes that every essential component is covered. Neither conclusion follows from the sentence alone. 'Not all' excludes universal coverage but does not identify the covered subset or guarantee that it is non-empty in this context. The operative schedule must specify the components and conditions. The ambiguity is practically important: fluent paraphrase can make an interpretation sound settled even when the underlying entitlement remains unspecified.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Scope determines what a negation, quantifier or modifier applies to. Not all is not equivalent to none. Only can focus on different elements depending on position and context. When an ambiguous phrase affects a decision, reformulate each reading and request clarification instead of silently choosing the convenient one.","description":"Resolve syntactic and pragmatic ambiguity without inventing certainty. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the summary clearly exclude?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Universal replacement coverage"},{"id":"o1","label":"Every possible replacement"},{"id":"o2","label":"The existence of a provider"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Not all denies all-inclusive coverage without specifying the actual covered subset.","passage":"A contract summary says that the provider will not replace all damaged components. One manager reads this as a refusal to replace any; another assumes that every essential component is covered. Neither conclusion follows from the sentence alone. 'Not all' excludes universal coverage but does not identify the covered subset or guarantee that it is non-empty in this context. The operative schedule must specify the components and conditions. The ambiguity is practically important: fluent paraphrase can make an interpretation sound settled even when the underlying entitlement remains unspecified.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is needed to resolve the practical issue?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A confident guess"},{"id":"o1","label":"A schedule naming components and conditions"},{"id":"o2","label":"A repetition of the same ambiguous sentence"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"An explicit operative schedule establishes the entitlement.","passage":"A contract summary says that the provider will not replace all damaged components. One manager reads this as a refusal to replace any; another assumes that every essential component is covered. Neither conclusion follows from the sentence alone. 'Not all' excludes universal coverage but does not identify the covered subset or guarantee that it is non-empty in this context. The operative schedule must specify the components and conditions. The ambiguity is practically important: fluent paraphrase can make an interpretation sound settled even when the underlying entitlement remains unspecified.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Scope determines what a negation, quantifier or modifier applies to. Not all is not equivalent to none. Only can focus on different elements depending on position and context. When an ambiguous phrase affects a decision, reformulate each reading and request clarification instead of silently choosing the convenient one.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the distinction: Not all is not equivalent ___ none.","solution":"to","explanation":"Equivalent to expresses sameness of meaning; these expressions differ in scope.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Place only before the subject to focus on the person: Maya approved the draft. Begin: Only...","solution":"Only Maya approved the draft.","explanation":"Only before Maya excludes other approvers, not other objects of approval.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Scope determines what a negation, quantifier or modifier applies to. Not all is not equivalent to none. Only can focus on different elements depending on position and context. When an ambiguous phrase affects a decision, reformulate each reading and request clarification instead of silently choosing the convenient one.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the precise intervention.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"That changes the scope; we need the coverage schedule before stating which components are excluded."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, not all always means none."},{"id":"o2","label":"Every essential component must be covered."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response identifies the scope shift and seeks the governing detail.","passage":"Manager: It says 'not all', so we can tell clients that none will be replaced. You want to stop an unjustified paraphrase.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Scope determines what a negation, quantifier or modifier applies to. Not all is not equivalent to none. Only can focus on different elements depending on position and context. When an ambiguous phrase affects a decision, reformulate each reading and request clarification instead of silently choosing the convenient one.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What distinction is being made?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Partial approval versus wholesale rejection"},{"id":"o1","label":"All recommendations versus identical recommendations"},{"id":"o2","label":"Approval versus a meeting date"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The speaker corrects a universal negative reading of partial non-approval.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-ambiguity"},{"prompt":"Why is one recommendation not being handled?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It is outside their remit."},{"id":"o1","label":"All recommendations are rejected."},{"id":"o2","label":"It has already been implemented."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The speaker says one falls outside their responsibility.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-ambiguity"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the summary clearly exclude?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Universal replacement coverage"},{"id":"o1","label":"Every possible replacement"},{"id":"o2","label":"The existence of a provider"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Not all denies all-inclusive coverage without specifying the actual covered subset.","passage":"A contract summary says that the provider will not replace all damaged components. One manager reads this as a refusal to replace any; another assumes that every essential component is covered. Neither conclusion follows from the sentence alone. 'Not all' excludes universal coverage but does not identify the covered subset or guarantee that it is non-empty in this context. The operative schedule must specify the components and conditions. The ambiguity is practically important: fluent paraphrase can make an interpretation sound settled even when the underlying entitlement remains unspecified.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What is needed to resolve the practical issue?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"A confident guess"},{"id":"o1","label":"A schedule naming components and conditions"},{"id":"o2","label":"A repetition of the same ambiguous sentence"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"An explicit operative schedule establishes the entitlement.","passage":"A contract summary says that the provider will not replace all damaged components. One manager reads this as a refusal to replace any; another assumes that every essential component is covered. Neither conclusion follows from the sentence alone. 'Not all' excludes universal coverage but does not identify the covered subset or guarantee that it is non-empty in this context. The operative schedule must specify the components and conditions. The ambiguity is practically important: fluent paraphrase can make an interpretation sound settled even when the underlying entitlement remains unspecified.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the precise intervention.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"That changes the scope; we need the coverage schedule before stating which components are excluded."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, not all always means none."},{"id":"o2","label":"Every essential component must be covered."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response identifies the scope shift and seeks the governing detail.","passage":"Manager: It says 'not all', so we can tell clients that none will be replaced. You want to stop an unjustified paraphrase.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Scope determines what a negation, quantifier or modifier applies to. Not all is not equivalent to none. Only can focus on different elements depending on position and context. When an ambiguous phrase affects a decision, reformulate each reading and request clarification instead of silently choosing the convenient one.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c2-precision","level":"C2","title":"Lexical precision and evaluative force","objective":"Select near-synonyms according to implication, collocation and evidential force.","project_prompt":"Write 300–450 words critically reformulating the unit argument for a different audience. Preserve its qualifications, analyse an alternative reading, and justify precise lexical and rhetorical choices.\nTopic: Lexical precision and evaluative force\nFocus: Select near-synonyms according to implication, collocation and evidential force.\nSource context: A commentary says the researcher 'conceded' that the data were incomplete and that a later response 'refuted' her explanation. The interview, however, shows her identifying the limitation at the outset, not yielding it under pressure. The response proposes an alternative mechanism but provides no decisive test between the two accounts. 'Acknowledged' and 'challenged' would therefore be more defensible choices. The original wording does not merely add colour: it recasts open methodological caution as retreat and unresolved disagreement as settled defeat.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Near-synonyms differ in commitment and evaluation. Concede suggests reluctant acceptance; acknowledge need not imply reluctance. Refute claims to disprove, whereas rebut can mean offering a counterargument. Plausible is not synonymous with proven. Precision requires preserving these distinctions, especially when compressing another person's position.","description":"Select near-synonyms according to implication, collocation and evidential force. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Why is 'conceded' questionable here?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The limitation was volunteered, not reluctantly yielded."},{"id":"o1","label":"The data were complete."},{"id":"o2","label":"The researcher never spoke."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The interview provides no evidence of the reluctance or reversal suggested by conceded.","passage":"A commentary says the researcher 'conceded' that the data were incomplete and that a later response 'refuted' her explanation. The interview, however, shows her identifying the limitation at the outset, not yielding it under pressure. The response proposes an alternative mechanism but provides no decisive test between the two accounts. 'Acknowledged' and 'challenged' would therefore be more defensible choices. The original wording does not merely add colour: it recasts open methodological caution as retreat and unresolved disagreement as settled defeat.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Why is 'refuted' too strong?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"There is no decisive discrimination between mechanisms."},{"id":"o1","label":"Any response proves an explanation false."},{"id":"o2","label":"Alternative mechanisms are forbidden."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"A counterproposal without a decisive test does not establish disproof.","passage":"A commentary says the researcher 'conceded' that the data were incomplete and that a later response 'refuted' her explanation. The interview, however, shows her identifying the limitation at the outset, not yielding it under pressure. The response proposes an alternative mechanism but provides no decisive test between the two accounts. 'Acknowledged' and 'challenged' would therefore be more defensible choices. The original wording does not merely add colour: it recasts open methodological caution as retreat and unresolved disagreement as settled defeat.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Near-synonyms differ in commitment and evaluation. Concede suggests reluctant acceptance; acknowledge need not imply reluctance. Refute claims to disprove, whereas rebut can mean offering a counterargument. Plausible is not synonymous with proven. Precision requires preserving these distinctions, especially when compressing another person's position.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete the collocation: The evidence falls short ___ establishing causation.","solution":"of","explanation":"Fall short of means fail to reach the required standard.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Use the requested reporting verb in past simple: She ___ (acknowledge) the limitation at the outset.","solution":"acknowledged","explanation":"Acknowledged reports recognition without necessarily implying reluctant concession.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Near-synonyms differ in commitment and evaluation. Concede suggests reluctant acceptance; acknowledge need not imply reluctance. Refute claims to disprove, whereas rebut can mean offering a counterargument. Plausible is not synonymous with proven. Precision requires preserving these distinctions, especially when compressing another person's position.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the principled response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Keep the stronger phrase because vividness overrides accuracy."},{"id":"o1","label":"The punchier wording would turn an unresolved challenge into an unsupported claim of defeat."},{"id":"o2","label":"Remove every evaluative verb from English."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The response explains the change in epistemic force rather than objecting to style generally.","passage":"Editor: 'Destroyed the argument' is punchier than 'raised an unresolved objection'. The evidence supports only the latter.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Near-synonyms differ in commitment and evaluation. Concede suggests reluctant acceptance; acknowledge need not imply reluctance. Refute claims to disprove, whereas rebut can mean offering a counterargument. Plausible is not synonymous with proven. Precision requires preserving these distinctions, especially when compressing another person's position.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What does 'compatible with' avoid claiming?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"That the mechanism is the unique explanation"},{"id":"o1","label":"That the findings exist"},{"id":"o2","label":"That concern is warranted"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Compatibility leaves competing explanations open.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-precision"},{"prompt":"Why does the speaker use cautious language?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The issue is trivial."},{"id":"o1","label":"The evidence limits certainty."},{"id":"o2","label":"The findings have been withdrawn."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"Caution is justified by evidential limits, not lack of importance.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-precision"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Why is 'conceded' questionable here?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The limitation was volunteered, not reluctantly yielded."},{"id":"o1","label":"The data were complete."},{"id":"o2","label":"The researcher never spoke."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The interview provides no evidence of the reluctance or reversal suggested by conceded.","passage":"A commentary says the researcher 'conceded' that the data were incomplete and that a later response 'refuted' her explanation. The interview, however, shows her identifying the limitation at the outset, not yielding it under pressure. The response proposes an alternative mechanism but provides no decisive test between the two accounts. 'Acknowledged' and 'challenged' would therefore be more defensible choices. The original wording does not merely add colour: it recasts open methodological caution as retreat and unresolved disagreement as settled defeat.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Why is 'refuted' too strong?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"There is no decisive discrimination between mechanisms."},{"id":"o1","label":"Any response proves an explanation false."},{"id":"o2","label":"Alternative mechanisms are forbidden."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"A counterproposal without a decisive test does not establish disproof.","passage":"A commentary says the researcher 'conceded' that the data were incomplete and that a later response 'refuted' her explanation. The interview, however, shows her identifying the limitation at the outset, not yielding it under pressure. The response proposes an alternative mechanism but provides no decisive test between the two accounts. 'Acknowledged' and 'challenged' would therefore be more defensible choices. The original wording does not merely add colour: it recasts open methodological caution as retreat and unresolved disagreement as settled defeat.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the principled response.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Keep the stronger phrase because vividness overrides accuracy."},{"id":"o1","label":"The punchier wording would turn an unresolved challenge into an unsupported claim of defeat."},{"id":"o2","label":"Remove every evaluative verb from English."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The response explains the change in epistemic force rather than objecting to style generally.","passage":"Editor: 'Destroyed the argument' is punchier than 'raised an unresolved objection'. The evidence supports only the latter.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Near-synonyms differ in commitment and evaluation. Concede suggests reluctant acceptance; acknowledge need not imply reluctance. Refute claims to disprove, whereas rebut can mean offering a counterargument. Plausible is not synonymous with proven. Precision requires preserving these distinctions, especially when compressing another person's position.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c2-sources","level":"C2","title":"Conflicting accounts and interpretive frames","objective":"Reconcile apparently conflicting accounts while preserving genuine disagreement.","project_prompt":"Write 300–450 words critically reformulating the unit argument for a different audience. Preserve its qualifications, analyse an alternative reading, and justify precise lexical and rhetorical choices.\nTopic: Conflicting accounts and interpretive frames\nFocus: Reconcile apparently conflicting accounts while preserving genuine disagreement.\nSource context: One historian describes the reform as a decisive break; another stresses the persistence of older institutions. The first examines legal authority over a decade, the second everyday administration over a generation. Their claims can coexist at those different scales. Yet a genuine disagreement remains over which scale should dominate the overall interpretation. A synthesis that announces complete agreement would be as misleading as one claiming that the historians contradict each other on every fact. The productive comparison separates compatible observations from competing judgements about historical significance.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Sources may conflict in fact, definition, timescale or value judgement. Before declaring contradiction, align the units of comparison. A synthesis must not erase residual disagreement to create artificial consensus. Distinguish an explanation of why two judgements differ from a demonstration that either judgement is correct.","description":"Reconcile apparently conflicting accounts while preserving genuine disagreement. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Which difference helps reconcile the observations?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Different objects and timescales of analysis"},{"id":"o1","label":"One historian has no evidence"},{"id":"o2","label":"Identical definitions of every term"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Legal authority over a decade and administration over a generation may show different patterns.","passage":"One historian describes the reform as a decisive break; another stresses the persistence of older institutions. The first examines legal authority over a decade, the second everyday administration over a generation. Their claims can coexist at those different scales. Yet a genuine disagreement remains over which scale should dominate the overall interpretation. A synthesis that announces complete agreement would be as misleading as one claiming that the historians contradict each other on every fact. The productive comparison separates compatible observations from competing judgements about historical significance.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What genuine disagreement remains?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether any reform happened"},{"id":"o1","label":"Which scale should determine the overall interpretation"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether decades contain years"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The residual dispute concerns interpretive priority, not every factual observation.","passage":"One historian describes the reform as a decisive break; another stresses the persistence of older institutions. The first examines legal authority over a decade, the second everyday administration over a generation. Their claims can coexist at those different scales. Yet a genuine disagreement remains over which scale should dominate the overall interpretation. A synthesis that announces complete agreement would be as misleading as one claiming that the historians contradict each other on every fact. The productive comparison separates compatible observations from competing judgements about historical significance.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Sources may conflict in fact, definition, timescale or value judgement. Before declaring contradiction, align the units of comparison. A synthesis must not erase residual disagreement to create artificial consensus. Distinguish an explanation of why two judgements differ from a demonstration that either judgement is correct.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete with although or because: The observations are compatible, ___ the interpretations differ.","solution":"although","explanation":"Although introduces a concessive finite clause; the differing interpretations do not prevent compatible observations.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Rewrite with neither...nor: The accounts are not identical. They are not wholly contradictory. Begin: The accounts are neither...","solution":"The accounts are neither identical nor wholly contradictory.","explanation":"Neither...nor preserves the two rejected extremes.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Sources may conflict in fact, definition, timescale or value judgement. Before declaring contradiction, align the units of comparison. A synthesis must not erase residual disagreement to create artificial consensus. Distinguish an explanation of why two judgements differ from a demonstration that either judgement is correct.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the faithful synthesis.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Their observations can coexist, but they still differ over which scale should guide the overall judgement."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, they agree on every interpretive question."},{"id":"o2","label":"No two accounts can ever be compared."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response retains the distinction between compatible facts and competing significance judgements.","passage":"Moderator: So both historians really mean the same thing. You want to acknowledge compatibility without inventing consensus.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Sources may conflict in fact, definition, timescale or value judgement. Before declaring contradiction, align the units of comparison. A synthesis must not erase residual disagreement to create artificial consensus. Distinguish an explanation of why two judgements differ from a demonstration that either judgement is correct.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"Why do the forecasts partly diverge?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"They use different policy assumptions."},{"id":"o1","label":"They predict different centuries."},{"id":"o2","label":"They contain no assumptions."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The scenarios differ over implementation of reforms.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-sources"},{"prompt":"What remains unresolved?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether reforms will credibly be implemented"},{"id":"o1","label":"Whether either forecast has a condition"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether comparisons are possible"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Aligning assumptions does not establish the credibility of the reforms.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-sources"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"Which difference helps reconcile the observations?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Different objects and timescales of analysis"},{"id":"o1","label":"One historian has no evidence"},{"id":"o2","label":"Identical definitions of every term"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Legal authority over a decade and administration over a generation may show different patterns.","passage":"One historian describes the reform as a decisive break; another stresses the persistence of older institutions. The first examines legal authority over a decade, the second everyday administration over a generation. Their claims can coexist at those different scales. Yet a genuine disagreement remains over which scale should dominate the overall interpretation. A synthesis that announces complete agreement would be as misleading as one claiming that the historians contradict each other on every fact. The productive comparison separates compatible observations from competing judgements about historical significance.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"What genuine disagreement remains?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Whether any reform happened"},{"id":"o1","label":"Which scale should determine the overall interpretation"},{"id":"o2","label":"Whether decades contain years"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The residual dispute concerns interpretive priority, not every factual observation.","passage":"One historian describes the reform as a decisive break; another stresses the persistence of older institutions. The first examines legal authority over a decade, the second everyday administration over a generation. Their claims can coexist at those different scales. Yet a genuine disagreement remains over which scale should dominate the overall interpretation. A synthesis that announces complete agreement would be as misleading as one claiming that the historians contradict each other on every fact. The productive comparison separates compatible observations from competing judgements about historical significance.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the faithful synthesis.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Their observations can coexist, but they still differ over which scale should guide the overall judgement."},{"id":"o1","label":"Yes, they agree on every interpretive question."},{"id":"o2","label":"No two accounts can ever be compared."}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"The response retains the distinction between compatible facts and competing significance judgements.","passage":"Moderator: So both historians really mean the same thing. You want to acknowledge compatibility without inventing consensus.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Sources may conflict in fact, definition, timescale or value judgement. Before declaring contradiction, align the units of comparison. A synthesis must not erase residual disagreement to create artificial consensus. Distinguish an explanation of why two judgements differ from a demonstration that either judgement is correct.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]},{"key":"c2-reformulation","level":"C2","title":"Faithful reformulation and final synthesis","objective":"Reformulate dense arguments for different audiences while preserving qualifications.","project_prompt":"Write 300–450 words critically reformulating the unit argument for a different audience. Preserve its qualifications, analyse an alternative reading, and justify precise lexical and rhetorical choices.\nTopic: Faithful reformulation and final synthesis\nFocus: Reformulate dense arguments for different audiences while preserving qualifications.\nSource context: A review concludes that the intervention can be effective where institutions have sufficient implementation capacity, while warning that the available studies underrepresent fragile settings. A policy brief compresses this to 'the intervention is effective everywhere except where institutions fail'. The rewrite changes a conditional finding into a near-universal claim and turns an evidence gap into blame. A faithful brief would retain both the capacity condition and the uncertainty about underrepresented settings. It could simplify the terminology, but not convert lack of evidence into evidence of institutional fault.\nOptional oral rehearsal: explain your response aloud, then note one change you would make. No microphone recording or automatic oral assessment is performed.","project_rubric":"Self-review (not an automatic grade): 1. Task and audience: did I answer the task and preserve relevant facts? 2. Organisation: are ideas linked and easy to follow? 3. Language: did I apply the unit focus with suitable vocabulary and register? 4. Accuracy and revision: what did I correct, and what needs teacher feedback?\nUnit language focus: Expert reformulation changes expression without silently changing scope, attribution or certainty. Make the argumentative structure visible: claim, grounds, warrant, qualification and consequence. Compression can remove examples but must preserve decision-relevant exceptions. The ability to answer recognition tasks does not by itself establish spontaneous C2 production; use the material as a basis for teacher-led discussion and extended writing.","description":"Reformulate dense arguments for different audiences while preserving qualifications. Guided practice, not a CEFR certification.","activities":[{"title":"Read and discover","kind":"story_explorer","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"What is lost in the policy brief?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The intervention's name only"},{"id":"o1","label":"The conditional scope and uncertainty of the finding"},{"id":"o2","label":"A claim of universal failure in the review"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The brief universalises the finding and moralises an evidence gap.","passage":"A review concludes that the intervention can be effective where institutions have sufficient implementation capacity, while warning that the available studies underrepresent fragile settings. A policy brief compresses this to 'the intervention is effective everywhere except where institutions fail'. The rewrite changes a conditional finding into a near-universal claim and turns an evidence gap into blame. A faithful brief would retain both the capacity condition and the uncertainty about underrepresented settings. It could simplify the terminology, but not convert lack of evidence into evidence of institutional fault.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which reformulation is faithful?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It works everywhere; failures prove institutional fault."},{"id":"o1","label":"It can work where capacity is sufficient; evidence in fragile settings remains limited."},{"id":"o2","label":"It has been proven ineffective in all fragile settings."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"This preserves both the enabling condition and the stated evidential limitation.","passage":"A review concludes that the intervention can be effective where institutions have sufficient implementation capacity, while warning that the available studies underrepresent fragile settings. A policy brief compresses this to 'the intervention is effective everywhere except where institutions fail'. The rewrite changes a conditional finding into a near-universal claim and turns an evidence gap into blame. A faithful brief would retain both the capacity condition and the uncertainty about underrepresented settings. It could simplify the terminology, but not convert lack of evidence into evidence of institutional fault.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Expert reformulation changes expression without silently changing scope, attribution or certainty. Make the argumentative structure visible: claim, grounds, warrant, qualification and consequence. Compression can remove examples but must preserve decision-relevant exceptions. The ability to answer recognition tasks does not by itself establish spontaneous C2 production; use the material as a basis for teacher-led discussion and extended writing.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Grammar in context","kind":"grammar_rescue","skill":"grammar","questions":[{"prompt":"Complete: Absence of evidence should not be construed ___ evidence of fault.","solution":"as","explanation":"Construe as means interpret as; the sentence warns against that substitution.","options":[],"response":"text"},{"prompt":"Rewrite with only if, keeping normal clause order: It can work when sufficient capacity is a necessary condition. Begin: It can work only if...","solution":"It can work only if there is sufficient capacity.","explanation":"Only if marks capacity as necessary without guaranteeing success.","options":[],"response":"text"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Expert reformulation changes expression without silently changing scope, attribution or certainty. Make the argumentative structure visible: claim, grounds, warrant, qualification and consequence. Compression can remove examples but must preserve decision-relevant exceptions. The ability to answer recognition tasks does not by itself establish spontaneous C2 production; use the material as a basis for teacher-led discussion and extended writing.","instructions":"Follow the transformation exactly. Write the target sentence; case, repeated spaces and final punctuation are normalized. Other paraphrases are not automatically assessed."},{"title":"Respond with purpose","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"reading","questions":[{"prompt":"Choose the accurate plain-language version.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It always works unless people do it badly."},{"id":"o1","label":"It may work when organisations can carry it out properly, but we know less about places with fewer resources."},{"id":"o2","label":"No conclusion of any kind is possible."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The version simplifies terms without removing the condition or manufacturing blame.","passage":"A policymaker asks for a one-sentence explanation without technical jargon. You must retain the condition and the evidence gap.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":false,"content":"Expert reformulation changes expression without silently changing scope, attribution or certainty. Make the argumentative structure visible: claim, grounds, warrant, qualification and consequence. Compression can remove examples but must preserve decision-relevant exceptions. The ability to answer recognition tasks does not by itself establish spontaneous C2 production; use the material as a basis for teacher-led discussion and extended writing.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Listen for meaning","kind":"sound_detective","skill":"listening","questions":[{"prompt":"What should the summary preserve?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Every procedural step"},{"id":"o1","label":"The scope and uncertainty of the conclusion"},{"id":"o2","label":"Only the most optimistic outcome"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The speaker prioritises decision-relevant qualifications over procedural detail.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-reformulation"},{"prompt":"Which false opposition is rejected?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"Accessible language versus accurate qualification"},{"id":"o1","label":"Procedure versus equipment"},{"id":"o2","label":"Speech versus a written alphabet"}],"solution":"o0","explanation":"Accessibility and concision need not require absolute or misleading claims.","passage":"","audio_key":"course_c2-reformulation"}],"assessment":false,"content":"Listen to the separate original recording. Listen once for the situation, then again for details and speaker intention. Replay as needed. The voice is synthetic; no speech is recorded from you.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."},{"title":"Unit review","kind":"conversation_quest","skill":"mixed","questions":[{"prompt":"What is lost in the policy brief?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"The intervention's name only"},{"id":"o1","label":"The conditional scope and uncertainty of the finding"},{"id":"o2","label":"A claim of universal failure in the review"}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The brief universalises the finding and moralises an evidence gap.","passage":"A review concludes that the intervention can be effective where institutions have sufficient implementation capacity, while warning that the available studies underrepresent fragile settings. A policy brief compresses this to 'the intervention is effective everywhere except where institutions fail'. The rewrite changes a conditional finding into a near-universal claim and turns an evidence gap into blame. A faithful brief would retain both the capacity condition and the uncertainty about underrepresented settings. It could simplify the terminology, but not convert lack of evidence into evidence of institutional fault.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Which reformulation is faithful?","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It works everywhere; failures prove institutional fault."},{"id":"o1","label":"It can work where capacity is sufficient; evidence in fragile settings remains limited."},{"id":"o2","label":"It has been proven ineffective in all fragile settings."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"This preserves both the enabling condition and the stated evidential limitation.","passage":"A review concludes that the intervention can be effective where institutions have sufficient implementation capacity, while warning that the available studies underrepresent fragile settings. A policy brief compresses this to 'the intervention is effective everywhere except where institutions fail'. The rewrite changes a conditional finding into a near-universal claim and turns an evidence gap into blame. A faithful brief would retain both the capacity condition and the uncertainty about underrepresented settings. It could simplify the terminology, but not convert lack of evidence into evidence of institutional fault.","audio_key":""},{"prompt":"Choose the accurate plain-language version.","options":[{"id":"o0","label":"It always works unless people do it badly."},{"id":"o1","label":"It may work when organisations can carry it out properly, but we know less about places with fewer resources."},{"id":"o2","label":"No conclusion of any kind is possible."}],"solution":"o1","explanation":"The version simplifies terms without removing the condition or manufacturing blame.","passage":"A policymaker asks for a one-sentence explanation without technical jargon. You must retain the condition and the evidence gap.","audio_key":""}],"assessment":true,"content":"Expert reformulation changes expression without silently changing scope, attribution or certainty. Make the argumentative structure visible: claim, grounds, warrant, qualification and consequence. Compression can remove examples but must preserve decision-relevant exceptions. The ability to answer recognition tasks does not by itself establish spontaneous C2 production; use the material as a basis for teacher-led discussion and extended writing.","instructions":"Read the context and answer every question. Review the explanation after submitting."}]}]$course$::jsonb;
 pack jsonb; activity jsonb; question jsonb; option jsonb;
 uid uuid; lid uuid; aid uuid; qid uuid; n integer; p integer;
BEGIN
 FOR pack IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(course) LOOP
  INSERT INTO units(level_code,title,description,position)
   SELECT pack->>'level',pack->>'title',pack->>'description',COALESCE(max(position),0)+1
   FROM units WHERE level_code=pack->>'level' RETURNING id INTO uid;
  INSERT INTO unit_projects(unit_id,prompt,rubric) VALUES(uid,pack->>'project_prompt',pack->>'project_rubric');
  INSERT INTO educational_resources(key,title,kind,url,attribution)
   VALUES('course_'||(pack->>'key'),(pack->>'level')||' '||(pack->>'title'),'audio',
    '/audio/course-'||(pack->>'key')||'.wav','Original EnglishQuest script; synthetic eSpeak NG narration');
  n:=0;
  FOR activity IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(pack->'activities') LOOP
   n:=n+1;
   INSERT INTO lessons(unit_id,title,objective,content,position)
    VALUES(uid,activity->>'title',pack->>'objective',activity->>'content',n) RETURNING id INTO lid;
   INSERT INTO activities(lesson_id,title,kind,instructions,assessment,skill)
    VALUES(lid,activity->>'title',activity->>'kind',activity->>'instructions',
     (activity->>'assessment')::boolean,activity->>'skill') RETURNING id INTO aid;
   p:=0;
   FOR question IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(activity->'questions') LOOP
    p:=p+1;
    INSERT INTO questions(activity_id,prompt,explanation,solution,position,response,passage,audio_key)
     VALUES(aid,question->>'prompt',question->>'explanation',question->'solution',p,
      COALESCE(question->>'response',''),COALESCE(question->>'passage',''),NULLIF(question->>'audio_key','')) RETURNING id INTO qid;
    FOR option IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(question->'options') LOOP
     INSERT INTO answer_options(id,question_id,label) VALUES(option->>'id',qid,option->>'label');
    END LOOP;
   END LOOP;
  END LOOP;
 END LOOP;
 UPDATE cefr_levels SET available=true,description=CASE code
  WHEN 'A1' THEN 'Foundations: identity, routines, places, food, abilities and everyday plans.'
  WHEN 'A2' THEN 'Everyday interaction: past events, travel, comparisons, health, plans and experiences.'
  WHEN 'B1' THEN 'Independent practice: narratives, advice, work, community, media and decisions.'
  WHEN 'B2' THEN 'Extended practice: argument, evidence, negotiation, reporting, environment and culture.'
  WHEN 'C1' THEN 'Advanced practice: stance, implication, synthesis, register, evaluation and mediation.'
  WHEN 'C2' THEN 'Nuanced practice: rhetoric, ambiguity, irony, precision, conflicting sources and reformulation.'
 END || ' Guided route; completion is not CEFR accreditation.';
END $migration$;
COMMIT;
